(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("docker",{"docker/containers-and-images":{"html":"\u003cp>A feature works on a developer&#39;s laptop, passes staging, and breaks in production. The code is identical. The difference is the environment: a different library version, a different base operating system, a different setting. Containers exist to remove that difference by shipping the application together with everything it needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a container is and how it differs from a virtual machine\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How images are built from layers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The everyday \u003ccode>docker\u003c/code> commands\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why floating tags such as \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> cause surprises\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-container-is-an-isolated-process\">A container is an isolated process\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A container is \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> a small virtual machine. It is an ordinary Linux process that the kernel has isolated using two features:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Namespaces\u003c/strong> give the process its own view of processes, network, mounts and hostname.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>cgroups\u003c/strong> limit how much CPU, memory and I/O it can use.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Because containers share the host&#39;s kernel, they start in well under a second and use far less memory than a VM, which runs a full operating system on virtual hardware. The trade-off is weaker isolation: a kernel flaw can affect every container on the host, so security settings matter (see the last lesson in this module).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Virtual machine\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Container\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Includes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Guest operating system and kernel\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application and its libraries\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Start time\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds to minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Milliseconds to seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Isolation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Strong, hardware level\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Process level, shared kernel\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Typical size\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Gigabytes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Megabytes to a few hundred megabytes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"images-and-layers\">Images and layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>image\u003c/strong> is a read-only template. It is built from \u003cstrong>layers\u003c/strong>, where each instruction in a Dockerfile adds a layer on top of the last. A running \u003cstrong>container\u003c/strong> is an image plus a thin writable layer. When the container is removed, that writable layer disappears.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker pull nginx:1.27\ndocker history nginx:1.27 | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker pull nginx:1.27\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> nginx:1.27 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">IMAGE          CREATED       CREATED BY                                      SIZE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">a1b2c3d4e5f6   2 weeks ago   CMD [&quot;nginx&quot; &quot;-g&quot; &quot;daemon off;&quot;]                0B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt;missing&gt;      2 weeks ago   EXPOSE map[80/tcp:{}]                           0B\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt;missing&gt;      2 weeks ago   COPY docker-entrypoint.sh / # buildkit          4.62kB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is sample output; IDs and dates will differ. Layers are cached and shared. If ten images start from the same base, the base is stored once on disk, which is why pulls are fast after the first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"everyday-commands\">Everyday commands\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx:1.27\ndocker ps\ncurl -sI http://localhost:8080 | head -1\ndocker logs --tail 3 web\ndocker exec -it web sh\ndocker stop web &amp;&amp; docker rm web\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx:1.27\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker ps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CONTAINER ID   IMAGE        COMMAND                  STATUS         PORTS                  NAMES\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">7f3a9c1b2d4e   nginx:1.27   &quot;/docker-entrypoint.…&quot;   Up 5 seconds   0.0.0.0:8080-&gt;80/tcp   web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sI http://localhost:8080 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HTTP/1.1 200 OK\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker logs --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> 3 web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> -it web sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker stop web &amp;&amp; docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> web\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-d\u003c/code> runs in the background, \u003ccode>--name\u003c/code> gives the container a readable name, and \u003ccode>-p 8080:80\u003c/code> publishes container port 80 on host port 8080. \u003ccode>docker exec\u003c/code> starts an extra process \u003cstrong>inside\u003c/strong> a running container, which is your debugging shell. \u003ccode>docker rm\u003c/code> deletes the container and its writable layer. Add \u003ccode>--rm\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>docker run\u003c/code> for throwaway containers that clean up after themselves.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tags-are-labels-not-versions\">Tags are labels, not versions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An image reference has a repository, a \u003cstrong>tag\u003c/strong> and optionally a \u003cstrong>digest\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>nginx:1.27\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>nginx@sha256:...\u003c/code>. A tag is a movable label. The publisher can point \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> or even \u003ccode>1.27\u003c/code> at a new image at any time. A digest identifies exact content and never changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Reference\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Behaviour\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>myapp:latest\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Whatever was pushed most recently, which can differ per host\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>myapp:1.8.2\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A specific release, usually stable but still movable\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>myapp@sha256:9b2...\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exact bytes, immutable\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-servers-run-different-code-from-the-same-tag\">Incident: two servers run different code from the same tag\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Production runs \u003ccode>myapp:latest\u003c/code> on three hosts. After a release, one host serves the new behaviour and two serve the old, and nobody changed the deploy command.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> was moved by a build on Monday. \u003ccode>docker run\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>docker compose up\u003c/code> reuse an image already stored on the host and only pull when it is missing. Hosts that had pulled \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> before Monday kept the old image. Compare what each host actually runs:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker image inspect myapp:latest --format '{{.Id}} {{index .RepoDigests 0}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker image inspect myapp:latest --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{.Id}} {{index .RepoDigests 0}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sha256:3c1e... myapp@sha256:8f2a...\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The digests differ between hosts, which proves it. The cure is to deploy by an immutable reference:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker pull registry.example.com/myapp:1.8.2\ndocker run -d --name myapp registry.example.com/myapp:1.8.2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker pull registry.example.com/myapp:1.8.2\ndocker run -d --name myapp registry.example.com/myapp:1.8.2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Have your pipeline tag each build with a version or commit identifier, deploy by that tag (or digest), and treat \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> as a convenience for local experiments only. Rollback becomes trivial: run the previous tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing important data in the container&#39;s writable layer, which is lost on \u003ccode>docker rm\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a container like a VM: logging in to change things by hand instead of rebuilding the image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Publishing ports you do not need to the whole network\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-container-is-an-isolated-process","text":"A container is an isolated process"},{"id":"images-and-layers","text":"Images and layers"},{"id":"everyday-commands","text":"Everyday commands"},{"id":"tags-are-labels-not-versions","text":"Tags are labels, not versions"},{"id":"incident-two-servers-run-different-code-from-the-same-tag","text":"Incident: two servers run different code from the same tag"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker: get started","u":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/"},{"t":"Open Container Initiative","u":"https://opencontainers.org/about/overview/"},{"t":"namespaces(7) manual","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/namespaces.7.html"}],"text":"A feature works on a developer&#39;s laptop, passes staging, and breaks in production. The code is identical. The difference is the environment: a different library version, a different base operating system, a different setting. Containers exist to remove that difference by shipping the application together with everything it needs. What you will learn What a container is and how it differs from a virtual machine How images are built from layers The everyday docker commands Why floating tags such as latest cause surprises A container is an isolated process A container is not a small virtual machine. It is an ordinary Linux process that the kernel has isolated using two features: Namespaces give the process its own view of processes, network, mounts and hostname. cgroups limit how much CPU, memory and I/O it can use. Because containers share the host&#39;s kernel, they start in well under a second and use far less memory than a VM, which runs a full operating system on virtual hardware. The trade-off is weaker isolation: a kernel flaw can affect every container on the host, so security settings matter (see the last lesson in this module). Virtual machine Container Includes Guest operating system and kernel Application and its libraries Start time Seconds to minutes Milliseconds to seconds Isolation Strong, hardware level Process level, shared kernel Typical size Gigabytes Megabytes to a few hundred megabytes Images and layers An image is a read-only template. It is built from layers , where each instruction in a Dockerfile adds a layer on top of the last. A running container is an image plus a thin writable layer. When the container is removed, that writable layer disappears. $ docker pull nginx:1.27 $ docker history nginx:1.27 | head -4 IMAGE CREATED CREATED BY SIZE a1b2c3d4e5f6 2 weeks ago CMD [\"nginx\" \"-g\" \"daemon off;\"] 0B \u003cmissing> 2 weeks ago EXPOSE map[80/tcp:{}] 0B \u003cmissing> 2 weeks ago COPY docker-entrypoint.sh / # buildkit 4.62kB This is sample output; IDs and dates will differ. Layers are cached and shared. If ten images start from the same base, the base is stored once on disk, which is why pulls are fast after the first. Everyday commands $ docker run -d --name web -p 8080:80 nginx:1.27 $ docker ps CONTAINER ID IMAGE COMMAND STATUS PORTS NAMES 7f3a9c1b2d4e nginx:1.27 \"/docker-entrypoint.…\" Up 5 seconds 0.0.0.0:8080->80/tcp web $ curl -sI http://localhost:8080 | head -1 HTTP/1.1 200 OK $ docker logs -- tail 3 web $ docker exec -it web sh $ docker stop web && docker rm web -d runs in the background, --name gives the container a readable name, and -p 8080:80 publishes container port 80 on host port 8080. docker exec starts an extra process inside a running container, which is your debugging shell. docker rm deletes the container and its writable layer. Add --rm to docker run for throwaway containers that clean up after themselves. Tags are labels, not versions An image reference has a repository, a tag and optionally a digest : nginx:1.27 or nginx@sha256:... . A tag is a movable label. The publisher can point latest or even 1.27 at a new image at any time. A digest identifies exact content and never changes. Reference Behaviour myapp:latest Whatever was pushed most recently, which can differ per host myapp:1.8.2 A specific release, usually stable but still movable myapp@sha256:9b2... Exact bytes, immutable Incident: two servers run different code from the same tag Incident Production runs myapp:latest on three hosts. After a release, one host serves the new behaviour and two serve the old, and nobody changed the deploy command. latest was moved by a build on Monday. docker run and docker compose up reuse an image already stored on the host and only pull when it is missing. Hosts that had pulled latest before Monday kept the old image. Compare what each host actually runs: $ docker image inspect myapp:latest --format &#x27;{{.Id}} {{index .RepoDigests 0}}&#x27; sha256:3c1e... myapp@sha256:8f2a... The digests differ be"},"docker/writing-dockerfiles":{"html":"\u003cp>Every code change triggers a six-minute image build, because a dependency install runs again each time. The final image is 1.2 GB for an application that is a single 15 MB binary. Both are Dockerfile problems, and both have simple fixes once you understand how Docker builds images.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The core instructions and what each does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How layer caching works and how to order instructions for it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Multi-stage builds for small final images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to run as a non-root user\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-core-instructions\">The core instructions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Instruction\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>FROM\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The base image to start from\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>WORKDIR\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Set the working directory for later steps\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>COPY\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Copy files from the build context into the image\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>RUN\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Execute a command at build time and save the result as a layer\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>ENV\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Set an environment variable\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>EXPOSE\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Document the listening port (it does not publish it)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>USER\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Switch to a non-root user\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>CMD\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>ENTRYPOINT\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>What runs when the container starts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>ENTRYPOINT\u003c/code> is the program, and \u003ccode>CMD\u003c/code> provides default arguments to it (or the whole command if there is no entrypoint). Use the \u003cstrong>exec form\u003c/strong> \u003ccode>[&quot;server&quot;, &quot;--port&quot;, &quot;8080&quot;]\u003c/code> so the program is PID 1 and receives stop signals directly, which allows graceful shutdown.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-cache-works\">How the cache works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Docker runs instructions in order and reuses a cached layer if the instruction and the files it depends on are unchanged. \u003cstrong>The first changed layer invalidates every layer after it.\u003c/strong> So put things that change rarely at the top and things that change often at the bottom.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Slow: any source edit invalidates the dependency install\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY . .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nCMD [&quot;python&quot;, &quot;app.py&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Slow: any source edit invalidates the dependency install\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> python:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.12\u003c/span>-slim\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /app\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CMD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;python&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app.py&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Fast: dependencies are reinstalled only when requirements.txt changes\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .\nCMD [&quot;python&quot;, &quot;app.py&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Fast: dependencies are reinstalled only when requirements.txt changes\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> python:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.12\u003c/span>-slim\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /app\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> requirements.txt .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CMD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;python&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app.py&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With the second version, editing \u003ccode>app.py\u003c/code> leaves the install layer cached:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker build -t shop-api:dev .\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker build -t shop-api:dev .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> =&gt; CACHED [2/5] WORKDIR /app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> =&gt; CACHED [3/5] COPY requirements.txt .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> =&gt; CACHED [4/5] RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> =&gt; [5/5] COPY . .\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is abbreviated sample output. A \u003ccode>.dockerignore\u003c/code> file keeps junk out of the build context and out of cache checks:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"node_modules\n.venv\n*.log\n.env\ntmp/\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">node_modules\n.venv\n*.log\n.env\ntmp/\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Never \u003ccode>COPY\u003c/code> secrets such as \u003ccode>.env\u003c/code> files into an image. Every layer is stored and can be read by anyone who can pull the image, even if a later step deletes the file.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multi-stage-builds\">Multi-stage builds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A build needs compilers and tools that the running application does not. A multi-stage build compiles in one stage and copies only the result into a small final stage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"FROM golang:1.23 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/server ./cmd/server\n\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot\nCOPY --from=build /out/server /server\nUSER nonroot\nEXPOSE 8080\nENTRYPOINT [&quot;/server&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> golang:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.23\u003c/span> AS build\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /src\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> go.mod go.sum ./\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> go mod download\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/server ./cmd/server\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> --from=build /out/server /server\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">USER\u003c/span> nonroot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">EXPOSE\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ENTRYPOINT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/server&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first stage includes the whole Go toolchain, which is hundreds of megabytes. The final stage contains only the compiled binary on a minimal base with no shell or package manager, so it is both small (often under 20 MB) and exposes far less to an attacker. For interpreted languages, use a \u003ccode>-slim\u003c/code> base and install only runtime dependencies in the final stage.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-bloated-slow-root-running-image\">Incident: a bloated, slow, root-running image\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The platform team scans the shop image and flags a long list of vulnerabilities in tools nobody uses. The image is 1.2 GB, pulls take minutes on new nodes, and the container runs as root.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Check what is inside:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker image ls shop-api\ndocker run --rm shop-api:old id\ndocker run --rm shop-api:new id\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker image \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> shop-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">REPOSITORY   TAG    IMAGE ID       SIZE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api     old    5d0c1e2a9b77   1.21GB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api     new    a4f7b8c3d1e0   18.4MB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> shop-api:old \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> shop-api:new \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">uid=65532(nonroot) gid=65532(nonroot)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The old image used a full build image as its runtime and never set \u003ccode>USER\u003c/code>. The fix is the multi-stage Dockerfile above. The benefits are measurable: a 98% smaller image, faster pulls and rollouts, fewer packages to patch, and a process that cannot write to most of the filesystem if it is compromised. Add a \u003cstrong>vulnerability scan\u003c/strong> (for example with Trivy) to your pipeline so a new finding fails the build instead of reaching production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-layers\">Predict the layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A Dockerfile copies the whole source before installing dependencies. You change one line of code. What happens at build time?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>FROM python:3.12\nCOPY . /app\nRUN pip install -r /app/requirements.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The copy layer changes, so pip install runs again and the build is slow.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Copy requirements.txt first and install, then copy the code. The dependency layer then stays cached when only code changes.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A secret is added in one layer and deleted in the next layer. Is it gone from the image?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>COPY key.pem /tmp/key.pem\nRUN rm /tmp/key.pem\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. It remains in the earlier layer and can be extracted.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Layers are additive. Use build secrets or a multi-stage build so the secret never lands in a layer.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Copying the whole source tree before installing dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> or an unpinned base image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running as root\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving build tools and caches in the final image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the shell form of \u003ccode>CMD\u003c/code>, which makes \u003ccode>/bin/sh\u003c/code> PID 1 and swallows stop signals\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-core-instructions","text":"The core instructions"},{"id":"how-the-cache-works","text":"How the cache works"},{"id":"multi-stage-builds","text":"Multi-stage builds"},{"id":"incident-a-bloated-slow-root-running-image","text":"Incident: a bloated, slow, root-running image"},{"id":"predict-the-layers","text":"Predict the layers"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Dockerfile reference","u":"https://docs.docker.com/reference/dockerfile/"},{"t":"Docker: build best practices","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/"},{"t":"Docker: multi-stage builds","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/"}],"text":"Every code change triggers a six-minute image build, because a dependency install runs again each time. The final image is 1.2 GB for an application that is a single 15 MB binary. Both are Dockerfile problems, and both have simple fixes once you understand how Docker builds images. What you will learn The core instructions and what each does How layer caching works and how to order instructions for it Multi-stage builds for small final images How to run as a non-root user The core instructions Instruction Purpose FROM The base image to start from WORKDIR Set the working directory for later steps COPY Copy files from the build context into the image RUN Execute a command at build time and save the result as a layer ENV Set an environment variable EXPOSE Document the listening port (it does not publish it) USER Switch to a non-root user CMD / ENTRYPOINT What runs when the container starts ENTRYPOINT is the program, and CMD provides default arguments to it (or the whole command if there is no entrypoint). Use the exec form [\"server\", \"--port\", \"8080\"] so the program is PID 1 and receives stop signals directly, which allows graceful shutdown. How the cache works Docker runs instructions in order and reuses a cached layer if the instruction and the files it depends on are unchanged. The first changed layer invalidates every layer after it. So put things that change rarely at the top and things that change often at the bottom. # Slow: any source edit invalidates the dependency install FROM python: 3.12 -slim WORKDIR /app COPY . . RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt CMD [ \"python\" , \"app.py\" ] # Fast: dependencies are reinstalled only when requirements.txt changes FROM python: 3.12 -slim WORKDIR /app COPY requirements.txt . RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt COPY . . CMD [ \"python\" , \"app.py\" ] With the second version, editing app.py leaves the install layer cached: $ docker build -t shop-api:dev . => CACHED [2/5] WORKDIR /app => CACHED [3/5] COPY requirements.txt . => CACHED [4/5] RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt => [5/5] COPY . . This is abbreviated sample output. A .dockerignore file keeps junk out of the build context and out of cache checks: node_modules .venv *.log .env tmp/ Warning Never COPY secrets such as .env files into an image. Every layer is stored and can be read by anyone who can pull the image, even if a later step deletes the file. Multi-stage builds A build needs compilers and tools that the running application does not. A multi-stage build compiles in one stage and copies only the result into a small final stage. FROM golang: 1.23 AS build WORKDIR /src COPY go.mod go.sum ./ RUN go mod download COPY . . RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/server ./cmd/server FROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot COPY --from=build /out/server /server USER nonroot EXPOSE 8080 ENTRYPOINT [ \"/server\" ] The first stage includes the whole Go toolchain, which is hundreds of megabytes. The final stage contains only the compiled binary on a minimal base with no shell or package manager, so it is both small (often under 20 MB) and exposes far less to an attacker. For interpreted languages, use a -slim base and install only runtime dependencies in the final stage. Incident: a bloated, slow, root-running image Incident The platform team scans the shop image and flags a long list of vulnerabilities in tools nobody uses. The image is 1.2 GB, pulls take minutes on new nodes, and the container runs as root. Check what is inside: $ docker image ls shop-api REPOSITORY TAG IMAGE ID SIZE shop-api old 5d0c1e2a9b77 1.21GB shop-api new a4f7b8c3d1e0 18.4MB $ docker run -- rm shop-api:old id uid=0(root) gid=0(root) groups=0(root) $ docker run -- rm shop-api:new id uid=65532(nonroot) gid=65532(nonroot) The old image used a full build image as its runtime and never set USER . The fix is the multi-stage Dockerfile above. The benefits are measurable: a 98% smaller image, faster pulls and rollouts, fewer "},"docker/volumes-and-networking":{"html":"\u003cp>A containerized app needs two things the container itself does not give you: a place to keep data that survives a restart, and a reliable way to find other containers. Get either wrong and you either lose data or spend an evening debugging why \u003ccode>db\u003c/code> does not resolve.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The three ways to persist or share data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How Docker networks and container DNS work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why the default network behaves differently from one you create\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to publish ports safely\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"persisting-data\">Persisting data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A container&#39;s writable layer is deleted with the container. Use one of these instead:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Created with\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Lives\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use for\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Named volume\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>-v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Managed by Docker\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Databases and other app state\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Bind mount\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>-v /srv/config:/etc/app:ro\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A host path you choose\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Config files, local development code\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>tmpfs\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--tmpfs /tmp\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Memory only\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Scratch data that must not touch disk\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker volume create pgdata\ndocker run -d --name db -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine\ndocker rm -f db\ndocker run -d --name db2 -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine\ndocker volume ls\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker volume create pgdata\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run -d --name db -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -f db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run -d --name db2 -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker volume \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">DRIVER    VOLUME NAME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">local     pgdata\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The second database starts with the first one&#39;s data because the volume outlived the container. Back up a volume by running a throwaway container that mounts it and archives the contents, and \u003cstrong>test restores\u003c/strong>, as you learned in the scripting module.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-note\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Note\u003c/div>\u003cp>Bind mounts expose host paths directly. Add \u003ccode>:ro\u003c/code> for read-only wherever the container does not need to write, and remember that file ownership inside the container must match the host&#39;s numeric user ID.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"networks-and-dns\">Networks and DNS\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Containers on the same \u003cstrong>network\u003c/strong> can reach each other. Docker creates a default network called \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code>, but it is best to create your own.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker network create backend\ndocker run -d --name db --network backend -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine\ndocker run --rm --network backend postgres:16-alpine pg_isready -h db\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker network create backend\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run -d --name db --network backend -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> --network backend postgres:16-alpine pg_isready -h db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db:5432 - accepting connections\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>On a \u003cstrong>user-defined network\u003c/strong> Docker runs an embedded DNS server, so containers find each other \u003cstrong>by name\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>db\u003c/code>). On the \u003cstrong>default bridge\u003c/strong> network there is no automatic name resolution between containers, only IP addresses, which change when containers restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Default \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>User-defined bridge\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Name resolution\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Yes, by container or service name\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Isolation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>All containers share it\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Only members can talk\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Recommended\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Quick experiments\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Anything real\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"publishing-ports\">Publishing ports\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Containers are reachable from other containers on their network without publishing anything. Publish a port only for traffic that must come from outside Docker.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx:1.27                 # all host interfaces\ndocker run -d -p 127.0.0.1:5432:5432 postgres:16    # localhost only\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx:1.27                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># all host interfaces\u003c/span>\ndocker run -d -p 127.0.0.1:5432:5432 postgres:16    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># localhost only\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>-p 5432:5432\u003c/code> makes the database reachable from every network interface of the host. Docker also edits firewall rules itself, which can bypass a host firewall you thought was protecting you. Bind to \u003ccode>127.0.0.1\u003c/code> or do not publish the port at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-app-cannot-find-the-database\">Incident: the app cannot find the database\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The app container logs \u003ccode>could not translate host name &quot;db&quot; to address: Name or service not known\u003c/code>. Both containers are running.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Check which network each container is on:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker inspect -f '{{range $k, $v := .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{$k}} {{end}}' app db\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect -f \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{range $k, $v := .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{$k}} {{end}}&#x27;\u003c/span> app db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">bridge\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">backend\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>app\u003c/code> is on the default \u003ccode>bridge\u003c/code> network and \u003ccode>db\u003c/code> is on \u003ccode>backend\u003c/code>. They cannot see each other at all, and even if they shared the default bridge, name lookup would not work there. Put both on the same user-defined network:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker network connect backend app\ndocker exec app getent hosts db\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker network connect backend app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> app getent hosts db\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">172.19.0.2      db\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The command \u003ccode>getent hosts db\u003c/code> inside the app container proves the name now resolves. Make this permanent in the run command (\u003ccode>--network backend\u003c/code>) or, better, define the network in Compose (next lesson) so nobody has to connect things by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"ctr&gt;vol;ctr&gt;bind;ctr&gt;net;net&gt;host\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where container data and traffic live\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctr\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Container\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"vol\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Volume\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bind\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bind mount\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Bridge network\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"host\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Host and port\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Container to Volume; 2, Container to Bind mount; 3, Container to Bridge network; 4, Bridge network to Host and port.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctr\">\u003cdt>Container \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A writable layer that disappears with the container.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Data written here is lost when the container is removed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"vol\">\u003cdt>Volume \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Managed storage that outlives the container.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not mounted means no persistence.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bind\">\u003cdt>Bind mount \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A host folder mounted inside.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong permissions or path on the host break the app.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Bridge network \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Containers on it reach each other by name; the host publishes ports.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A container on another network cannot resolve the name.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"host\">\u003cdt>Host and port \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A published port maps to the container port.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Two containers cannot publish the same host port.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keeping database files inside the container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Publishing database ports to the whole host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on the default bridge network and container IP addresses\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Bind-mounting source code in production instead of baking it into the image\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"persisting-data","text":"Persisting data"},{"id":"networks-and-dns","text":"Networks and DNS"},{"id":"publishing-ports","text":"Publishing ports"},{"id":"incident-the-app-cannot-find-the-database","text":"Incident: the app cannot find the database"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker: volumes","u":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/storage/volumes/"},{"t":"Docker: networking","u":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/network/"},{"t":"cgroups(7) manual","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/cgroups.7.html"}],"text":"A containerized app needs two things the container itself does not give you: a place to keep data that survives a restart, and a reliable way to find other containers. Get either wrong and you either lose data or spend an evening debugging why db does not resolve. What you will learn The three ways to persist or share data How Docker networks and container DNS work Why the default network behaves differently from one you create How to publish ports safely Persisting data A container&#39;s writable layer is deleted with the container. Use one of these instead: Type Created with Lives Use for Named volume -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data Managed by Docker Databases and other app state Bind mount -v /srv/config:/etc/app:ro A host path you choose Config files, local development code tmpfs --tmpfs /tmp Memory only Scratch data that must not touch disk $ docker volume create pgdata $ docker run -d --name db -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine $ docker rm -f db $ docker run -d --name db2 -v pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine $ docker volume ls DRIVER VOLUME NAME local pgdata The second database starts with the first one&#39;s data because the volume outlived the container. Back up a volume by running a throwaway container that mounts it and archives the contents, and test restores , as you learned in the scripting module. Note Bind mounts expose host paths directly. Add :ro for read-only wherever the container does not need to write, and remember that file ownership inside the container must match the host&#39;s numeric user ID. Networks and DNS Containers on the same network can reach each other. Docker creates a default network called bridge , but it is best to create your own. $ docker network create backend $ docker run -d --name db --network backend -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=change-me postgres:16-alpine $ docker run -- rm --network backend postgres:16-alpine pg_isready -h db db:5432 - accepting connections On a user-defined network Docker runs an embedded DNS server, so containers find each other by name ( db ). On the default bridge network there is no automatic name resolution between containers, only IP addresses, which change when containers restart. Default bridge User-defined bridge Name resolution No Yes, by container or service name Isolation All containers share it Only members can talk Recommended Quick experiments Anything real Publishing ports Containers are reachable from other containers on their network without publishing anything. Publish a port only for traffic that must come from outside Docker. docker run -d -p 8080:80 nginx:1.27 # all host interfaces docker run -d -p 127.0.0.1:5432:5432 postgres:16 # localhost only Warning -p 5432:5432 makes the database reachable from every network interface of the host. Docker also edits firewall rules itself, which can bypass a host firewall you thought was protecting you. Bind to 127.0.0.1 or do not publish the port at all. Incident: the app cannot find the database Incident The app container logs could not translate host name \"db\" to address: Name or service not known . Both containers are running. Check which network each container is on: $ docker inspect -f &#x27;{{range $k, $v := .NetworkSettings.Networks}}{{$k}} {{end}}&#x27; app db bridge backend app is on the default bridge network and db is on backend . They cannot see each other at all, and even if they shared the default bridge, name lookup would not work there. Put both on the same user-defined network: $ docker network connect backend app $ docker exec app getent hosts db 172.19.0.2 db The command getent hosts db inside the app container proves the name now resolves. Make this permanent in the run command ( --network backend ) or, better, define the network in Compose (next lesson) so nobody has to connect things by hand. Where container data and traffic live Flow in order: 1, Container to Volume; 2, Container to Bind "},"docker/compose":{"html":"\u003cp>Running an app with a database means a network, a volume, a password, a start order and about five \u003ccode>docker run\u003c/code> commands that someone must remember in the right order. Docker Compose captures all of that in one file that you can review, version and run with one command.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The structure of a Compose file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks and start-up ordering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets and environment variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Everyday Compose commands\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-complete-example\">A complete example\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"services:\n  app:\n    build: .\n    ports:\n      - &quot;8080:8080&quot;\n    environment:\n      DB_HOST: db\n      DB_USER: app\n      DB_NAME: appdb\n      DB_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password\n    secrets:\n      - db_password\n    depends_on:\n      db:\n        condition: service_healthy\n    networks:\n      - backend\n\n  db:\n    image: postgres:16-alpine\n    environment:\n      POSTGRES_USER: app\n      POSTGRES_DB: appdb\n      POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password\n    secrets:\n      - db_password\n    volumes:\n      - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\n    healthcheck:\n      test: [&quot;CMD-SHELL&quot;, &quot;pg_isready -U app -d appdb&quot;]\n      interval: 5s\n      timeout: 3s\n      retries: 10\n    networks:\n      - backend\n\nsecrets:\n  db_password:\n    file: ./secrets/db_password.txt\n\nvolumes:\n  pgdata:\n\nnetworks:\n  backend:\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">services:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;8080:8080&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">DB_HOST:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">DB_USER:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">DB_NAME:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">appdb\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">DB_PASSWORD_FILE:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/run/secrets/db_password\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secrets:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db_password\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">depends_on:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">db:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">condition:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">service_healthy\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">networks:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">backend\u003c/span>\n\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">db:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">postgres:16-alpine\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">POSTGRES_USER:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">app\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">POSTGRES_DB:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">appdb\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/run/secrets/db_password\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secrets:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">db_password\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">healthcheck:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;CMD-SHELL&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pg_isready -U app -d appdb&quot;\u003c/span>]\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">interval:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">5s\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">timeout:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">3s\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">retries:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">networks:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">backend\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secrets:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">db_password:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">file:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./secrets/db_password.txt\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">volumes:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pgdata:\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">networks:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">backend:\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Read it top to bottom. Each entry under \u003ccode>services\u003c/code> becomes a container. Compose creates the \u003ccode>backend\u003c/code> network and the \u003ccode>pgdata\u003c/code> volume, and services reach each other by their \u003cstrong>service name\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>db\u003c/code>). Notice there is no \u003ccode>version:\u003c/code> line. Current Compose ignores that key and warns that it is obsolete.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Secrets\u003c/strong> are mounted as files under \u003ccode>/run/secrets/\u003c/code> inside the containers that list them, so the password never appears in \u003ccode>docker inspect\u003c/code> output or in the image. The \u003ccode>postgres\u003c/code> image reads \u003ccode>POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE\u003c/code> natively. Your own application must read the file named by \u003ccode>DB_PASSWORD_FILE\u003c/code> for this to work, which is a small code change worth making.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-checks-and-ordering\">Health checks and ordering\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>depends_on\u003c/code> alone only waits for the container to \u003cem>start\u003c/em>, not for the program inside to be \u003cem>ready\u003c/em>. A database takes several seconds to accept connections. Adding a \u003ccode>healthcheck\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>condition: service_healthy\u003c/code> makes Compose wait until \u003ccode>pg_isready\u003c/code> succeeds before it starts \u003ccode>app\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"everyday-commands\">Everyday commands\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker compose up -d --build\ndocker compose ps\ndocker compose logs -f --tail 20 app\ndocker compose exec db psql -U app -d appdb -c 'select 1'\ndocker compose down            # stops and removes containers and networks, keeps volumes\ndocker compose down -v         # also deletes volumes: this destroys the data\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose up -d --build\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose ps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            IMAGE           COMMAND                  SERVICE   STATUS                    PORTS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-app-1      shop-app        &quot;/server&quot;                app       Up 12 seconds             0.0.0.0:8080-&gt;8080/tcp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-db-1       postgres:16...  &quot;docker-entrypoint.s…&quot;   db        Up 17 seconds (healthy)   5432/tcp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose logs -f --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> 20 app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> db psql -U app -d appdb -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;select 1&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose down            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># stops and removes containers and networks, keeps volumes\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose down -v         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># also deletes volumes: this destroys the data\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is sample output. \u003ccode>up -d --build\u003c/code> rebuilds changed images and starts everything in the background. \u003ccode>exec\u003c/code> runs a command in a running service. \u003ccode>down -v\u003c/code> is the dangerous one: it removes named volumes, so use it deliberately.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>For different environments, keep one base file and override pieces with \u003ccode>-f compose.prod.yaml\u003c/code>, or use \u003cstrong>profiles\u003c/strong> so optional services (a debug tool, a mail catcher) start only when requested:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"  mailcatcher:\n    image: sj26/mailcatcher\n    profiles: [&quot;dev&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">mailcatcher:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sj26/mailcatcher\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">profiles:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dev&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>docker compose --profile dev up -d\u003c/code> includes it, and a plain \u003ccode>up -d\u003c/code> leaves it out.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-app-crashes-on-every-first-start\">Incident: the app crashes on every first start\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After \u003ccode>docker compose up -d\u003c/code>, the app container exits with a connection error and restarts a few times before it works. On a slow laptop it never recovers.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker compose logs app | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose logs app | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">app-1  | 2026/10/24 03:12:01 connect to database: dial tcp 172.20.0.2:5432: connect: connection refused\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">app-1  | 2026/10/24 03:12:01 exiting\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The app started before PostgreSQL finished initializing, and it gives up on the first failed connection. There are two layers of fix. First, the Compose file&#39;s \u003ccode>healthcheck\u003c/code> plus \u003ccode>condition: service_healthy\u003c/code> removes the race at start-up. Second, the application should \u003cstrong>retry\u003c/strong> its connection with a short backoff, because databases restart and networks hiccup after start-up too. Relying on start order alone would leave it fragile whenever the database restarts later.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Confirm the ordering works:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker compose down &amp;&amp; docker compose up -d\ndocker compose ps --format '{{.Service}} {{.Status}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose down &amp;&amp; docker compose up -d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker compose ps --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{.Service}} {{.Status}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db Up 14 seconds (healthy)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">app Up 8 seconds\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>depends_on\u003c/code> without a health check and assuming the dependency is ready\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting passwords directly in the Compose file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running \u003ccode>docker compose down -v\u003c/code> out of habit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Expecting Compose to be a production orchestrator; it runs on one host and has no scheduling across machines\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-complete-example","text":"A complete example"},{"id":"health-checks-and-ordering","text":"Health checks and ordering"},{"id":"everyday-commands","text":"Everyday commands"},{"id":"incident-the-app-crashes-on-every-first-start","text":"Incident: the app crashes on every first start"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker Compose documentation","u":"https://docs.docker.com/compose/"},{"t":"Docker: get started","u":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/"},{"t":"Docker: build best practices","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/"}],"text":"Running an app with a database means a network, a volume, a password, a start order and about five docker run commands that someone must remember in the right order. Docker Compose captures all of that in one file that you can review, version and run with one command. What you will learn The structure of a Compose file Health checks and start-up ordering Secrets and environment variables Everyday Compose commands A complete example services: app: build: . ports: - \"8080:8080\" environment: DB_HOST: db DB_USER: app DB_NAME: appdb DB_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password secrets: - db_password depends_on: db: condition: service_healthy networks: - backend db: image: postgres:16-alpine environment: POSTGRES_USER: app POSTGRES_DB: appdb POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE: /run/secrets/db_password secrets: - db_password volumes: - pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data healthcheck: test: [ \"CMD-SHELL\" , \"pg_isready -U app -d appdb\" ] interval: 5s timeout: 3s retries: 10 networks: - backend secrets: db_password: file: ./secrets/db_password.txt volumes: pgdata: networks: backend: Read it top to bottom. Each entry under services becomes a container. Compose creates the backend network and the pgdata volume, and services reach each other by their service name ( db ). Notice there is no version: line. Current Compose ignores that key and warns that it is obsolete. Secrets are mounted as files under /run/secrets/ inside the containers that list them, so the password never appears in docker inspect output or in the image. The postgres image reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE natively. Your own application must read the file named by DB_PASSWORD_FILE for this to work, which is a small code change worth making. Health checks and ordering depends_on alone only waits for the container to start , not for the program inside to be ready . A database takes several seconds to accept connections. Adding a healthcheck and condition: service_healthy makes Compose wait until pg_isready succeeds before it starts app . Everyday commands $ docker compose up -d --build $ docker compose ps NAME IMAGE COMMAND SERVICE STATUS PORTS shop-app-1 shop-app \"/server\" app Up 12 seconds 0.0.0.0:8080->8080/tcp shop-db-1 postgres:16... \"docker-entrypoint.s…\" db Up 17 seconds (healthy) 5432/tcp $ docker compose logs -f -- tail 20 app $ docker compose exec db psql -U app -d appdb -c &#x27;select 1&#x27; $ docker compose down # stops and removes containers and networks, keeps volumes $ docker compose down -v # also deletes volumes: this destroys the data This is sample output. up -d --build rebuilds changed images and starts everything in the background. exec runs a command in a running service. down -v is the dangerous one: it removes named volumes, so use it deliberately. For different environments, keep one base file and override pieces with -f compose.prod.yaml , or use profiles so optional services (a debug tool, a mail catcher) start only when requested: mailcatcher: image: sj26/mailcatcher profiles: [ \"dev\" ] docker compose --profile dev up -d includes it, and a plain up -d leaves it out. Incident: the app crashes on every first start Incident After docker compose up -d , the app container exits with a connection error and restarts a few times before it works. On a slow laptop it never recovers. $ docker compose logs app | head -3 app-1 | 2026/10/24 03:12:01 connect to database: dial tcp 172.20.0.2:5432: connect: connection refused app-1 | 2026/10/24 03:12:01 exiting The app started before PostgreSQL finished initializing, and it gives up on the first failed connection. There are two layers of fix. First, the Compose file&#39;s healthcheck plus condition: service_healthy removes the race at start-up. Second, the application should retry its connection with a short backoff, because databases restart and networks hiccup after start-up too. Relying on start order alone would leave it fragile whenever the database restarts later. Confirm the ordering works: $ docker compose down && docker com"},"docker/registries-and-supply-chain":{"html":"\u003cp>An image is software you did not write, built from a base you did not audit, running with the privileges you give it. Registries store and distribute images, and a handful of habits keep what you pull and push trustworthy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How registries, repositories, tags and digests relate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tagging and pushing images, and why \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> is a trap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning images and producing a software bill of materials (SBOM)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Signing images and enforcing trust\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"names-tags-and-digests\">Names, tags and digests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A full image reference looks like \u003ccode>registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/code>. The registry is the server, \u003ccode>team/shop-api\u003c/code> is the repository, and \u003ccode>1.8.2\u003c/code> is a \u003cstrong>tag\u003c/strong>, a movable label. A tag can be repointed to different content at any time. A \u003cstrong>digest\u003c/strong> (\u003ccode>@sha256:...\u003c/code>) is a hash of the image content and never changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker build -t registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 .\ndocker login registry.example.com\ndocker push registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\ndocker pull registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\ndocker inspect --format '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\ndocker images --digests | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker build -t registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker login registry.example.com\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker push registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker pull registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{index .RepoDigests 0}}&#x27;\u003c/span> registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker images --digests | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deploy by digest when you need to be certain: \u003ccode>image: registry.example.com/team/shop-api@sha256:3b1c...\u003c/code>. Tag with the \u003cstrong>version\u003c/strong> and the \u003cstrong>build identifier\u003c/strong>, such as \u003ccode>1.8.2\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>1.8.2-b457\u003c/code>, and never deploy \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code>. It means &quot;whatever was pushed last&quot;, so two nodes can run different code under the same name.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-and-pinning-base-images\">Choosing and pinning base images\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prefer small, maintained bases: distroless, Alpine or a slim variant of an official image. Pin the version, and for stronger reproducibility the digest.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"FROM python:3.12-slim@sha256:0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef\nRUN useradd --create-home app\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .\nUSER app\nCMD [&quot;python&quot;, &quot;main.py&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> python:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.12\u003c/span>-slim@sha256:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0123456789\u003c/span>abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> useradd --create-home app\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /app\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> requirements.txt .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">USER\u003c/span> app\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">CMD\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;python&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;main.py&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Pinning means a rebuild gives the same base. It also means you must update the pin regularly, so use an automated dependency updater to propose new digests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scanning-and-sboms\">Scanning and SBOMs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A scanner compares the packages inside an image against vulnerability databases. An \u003cstrong>SBOM\u003c/strong> (software bill of materials) lists everything in the image so you can answer &quot;are we affected by this new CVE&quot; in minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\ntrivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\nsyft registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 -o spdx-json &gt; sbom.spdx.json\ngrype sbom:sbom.spdx.json --fail-on high\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>syft registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 -o spdx-json &gt; sbom.spdx.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grype sbom:sbom.spdx.json --fail-on high\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run a scan in the pipeline and fail the build on critical findings with a fix available. Scan again on a schedule, because new vulnerabilities are published for images that have not changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signing-and-verifying\">Signing and verifying\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Signing proves an image was built by your pipeline and has not been altered. Tools such as \u003cstrong>cosign\u003c/strong> (Sigstore) sign by digest and let the cluster verify before running.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cosign sign --key cosign.key registry.example.com/team/shop-api@sha256:3b1c\ncosign verify --key cosign.pub registry.example.com/team/shop-api@sha256:3b1c\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cosign sign --key cosign.key registry.example.com/team/shop-api@sha256:3b1c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>cosign verify --key cosign.pub registry.example.com/team/shop-api@sha256:3b1c\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>On the cluster side, an admission policy (for example Kyverno or a policy controller) rejects images that are unsigned or come from an unapproved registry. Without enforcement, signing is only documentation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"registry-hygiene\">Registry hygiene\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use a private registry or a pull-through cache for production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Authenticate with short-lived credentials or workload identity, not shared passwords\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Apply retention rules to delete old untagged images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Make production tags immutable so a tag cannot be overwritten\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mirror critical base images, so a public outage does not stop your builds\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-same-tag-two-behaviours\">Incident: the same tag, two behaviours\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A hotfix is pushed over \u003ccode>shop-api:1.8\u003c/code> to fix a bug. Half the nodes already had the old layer cached and keep running it, the other half pull the new image. Customers report inconsistent behaviour, and rollback cannot be confirmed because both versions carry the same tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Mutable tags make deployments unpredictable. Find which digest each Pod runs and move to immutable references:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath='{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{&quot;\\t&quot;}{.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}{&quot;\\n&quot;}{end}'\nkubectl set image deployment/shop-api api=registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.3 -n shop\nkubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{range .items[*]}{.metadata.name}{&quot;\\t&quot;}{.status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}{&quot;\\n&quot;}{end}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> image deployment/shop-api api=registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.3 -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Publish every build under a unique tag, enable tag immutability in the registry, and reference digests from the deployment manifest so the cluster can only run what the pipeline produced.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deploying \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> or any mutable tag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never rebuilding images, so base-image vulnerabilities pile up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning once at build time and never again\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Signing images but not verifying them at admission\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing a single registry password among all pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"names-tags-and-digests","text":"Names, tags and digests"},{"id":"choosing-and-pinning-base-images","text":"Choosing and pinning base images"},{"id":"scanning-and-sboms","text":"Scanning and SBOMs"},{"id":"signing-and-verifying","text":"Signing and verifying"},{"id":"registry-hygiene","text":"Registry hygiene"},{"id":"incident-the-same-tag-two-behaviours","text":"Incident: the same tag, two behaviours"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker Hub documentation","u":"https://docs.docker.com/docker-hub/"},{"t":"Trivy documentation","u":"https://trivy.dev/latest/docs/"},{"t":"Sigstore: Cosign","u":"https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/signing/overview/"}],"text":"An image is software you did not write, built from a base you did not audit, running with the privileges you give it. Registries store and distribute images, and a handful of habits keep what you pull and push trustworthy. What you will learn How registries, repositories, tags and digests relate Tagging and pushing images, and why latest is a trap Scanning images and producing a software bill of materials (SBOM) Signing images and enforcing trust Names, tags and digests A full image reference looks like registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 . The registry is the server, team/shop-api is the repository, and 1.8.2 is a tag , a movable label. A tag can be repointed to different content at any time. A digest ( @sha256:... ) is a hash of the image content and never changes. $ docker build -t registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 . $ docker login registry.example.com $ docker push registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 $ docker pull registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 $ docker inspect --format &#x27;{{index .RepoDigests 0}}&#x27; registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 $ docker images --digests | head -3 Deploy by digest when you need to be certain: image: registry.example.com/team/shop-api@sha256:3b1c... . Tag with the version and the build identifier , such as 1.8.2 and 1.8.2-b457 , and never deploy latest . It means \"whatever was pushed last\", so two nodes can run different code under the same name. Choosing and pinning base images Prefer small, maintained bases: distroless, Alpine or a slim variant of an official image. Pin the version, and for stronger reproducibility the digest. FROM python: 3.12 -slim@sha256: 0123456789 abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef RUN useradd --create-home app WORKDIR /app COPY requirements.txt . RUN python -m pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt COPY . . USER app CMD [ \"python\" , \"main.py\" ] Pinning means a rebuild gives the same base. It also means you must update the pin regularly, so use an automated dependency updater to propose new digests. Scanning and SBOMs A scanner compares the packages inside an image against vulnerability databases. An SBOM (software bill of materials) lists everything in the image so you can answer \"are we affected by this new CVE\" in minutes. $ trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 $ trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 $ syft registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 -o spdx-json > sbom.spdx.json $ grype sbom:sbom.spdx.json --fail-on high Run a scan in the pipeline and fail the build on critical findings with a fix available. Scan again on a schedule, because new vulnerabilities are published for images that have not changed. Signing and verifying Signing proves an image was built by your pipeline and has not been altered. Tools such as cosign (Sigstore) sign by digest and let the cluster verify before running. $ cosign sign --key cosign.key registry.example.com/team/shop-api@sha256:3b1c $ cosign verify --key cosign.pub registry.example.com/team/shop-api@sha256:3b1c On the cluster side, an admission policy (for example Kyverno or a policy controller) rejects images that are unsigned or come from an unapproved registry. Without enforcement, signing is only documentation. Registry hygiene Use a private registry or a pull-through cache for production Authenticate with short-lived credentials or workload identity, not shared passwords Apply retention rules to delete old untagged images Make production tags immutable so a tag cannot be overwritten Mirror critical base images, so a public outage does not stop your builds Incident: the same tag, two behaviours Incident A hotfix is pushed over shop-api:1.8 to fix a bug. Half the nodes already had the old layer cached and keep running it, the other half pull the new image. Customers report inconsistent behaviour, and rollback cannot be confirmed because both versions carry the same tag. Mutable tags"},"docker/runtimes-oci-and-podman":{"html":"\u003cp>Docker made containers popular, but Docker is not the only piece of software involved. Kubernetes does not need the Docker daemon at all. Knowing the layers helps you debug nodes, choose tools and read documentation that mentions containerd, runc or CRI.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The layers: CLI, engine, high-level runtime and low-level runtime\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What the OCI image and runtime specifications guarantee\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using Podman as a daemonless, rootless alternative\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspecting containers on a Kubernetes node with \u003ccode>crictl\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-layers\">The layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When you run \u003ccode>docker run\u003c/code>, several programs cooperate:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Layer\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Job\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>CLI\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>docker\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>podman\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>nerdctl\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Takes your command\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Engine / API\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>dockerd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Builds images, manages networks and volumes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>High-level runtime\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>containerd\u003c/code>, CRI-O\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Pulls images, manages container lifecycle\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Low-level runtime\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>runc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>crun\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Creates the Linux namespaces and cgroups and starts the process\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>At the bottom a container is just a Linux process started with \u003cstrong>namespaces\u003c/strong> (an isolated view of processes, network, mounts and users) and \u003cstrong>cgroups\u003c/strong> (limits on CPU and memory). \u003ccode>runc\u003c/code> is the reference tool that does exactly that.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-open-container-initiative\">The Open Container Initiative\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The OCI publishes two specifications. The \u003cstrong>image spec\u003c/strong> defines how an image is laid out: a manifest, a configuration and a stack of layer tarballs. The \u003cstrong>runtime spec\u003c/strong> defines how a runtime turns an unpacked image into a running process. Because of these standards, an image built with Docker runs on containerd, CRI-O or Podman, and Kubernetes can swap its runtime without changing your images.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker save shop-api:1.8.2 -o shop-api.tar\ntar -tf shop-api.tar | head\ndocker history shop-api:1.8.2\ndocker inspect shop-api:1.8.2 --format '{{.Config.Entrypoint}} {{.Config.Cmd}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker save shop-api:1.8.2 -o shop-api.tar\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>tar -tf shop-api.tar | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect shop-api:1.8.2 --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{.Config.Entrypoint}} {{.Config.Cmd}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"podman\">Podman\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Podman runs containers without a long-running daemon, and can do so as a normal user (\u003cstrong>rootless\u003c/strong>). Its commands are deliberately compatible with Docker&#39;s.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"podman run --rm -it docker.io/library/alpine:3.20 uname -a\npodman build -t shop-api:dev .\npodman ps\npodman generate systemd --new --name shop-api &gt; shop-api.service\npodman pod create --name shop -p 8080:80\npodman run -d --pod shop --name web docker.io/library/nginx:1.27\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>podman run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it docker.io/library/alpine:3.20 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uname\u003c/span> -a\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>podman build -t shop-api:dev .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>podman ps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>podman generate systemd --new --name shop-api &gt; shop-api.service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>podman pod create --name shop -p 8080:80\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>podman run -d --pod shop --name web docker.io/library/nginx:1.27\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>In rootless mode, &quot;root&quot; inside the container maps to your unprivileged user on the host. If an attacker escapes the container they land as an unprivileged user, which greatly limits damage. Podman can also run several containers in a \u003cstrong>pod\u003c/strong> that shares a network namespace, the same idea as a Kubernetes Pod, and can export or read Kubernetes YAML with \u003ccode>podman generate kube\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>podman play kube\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runtimes-under-kubernetes\">Runtimes under Kubernetes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Modern Kubernetes talks to a runtime through the \u003cstrong>Container Runtime Interface (CRI)\u003c/strong>. Most clusters use containerd or CRI-O. On a node there may be no \u003ccode>docker\u003c/code> command, so use \u003ccode>crictl\u003c/code> to see what the kubelet sees.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo crictl ps\nsudo crictl images\nsudo crictl logs 3f9a1c2b8d\nsudo crictl inspect 3f9a1c2b8d | head -30\nsudo crictl pods\nsystemctl status containerd\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl ps\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl images\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl logs 3f9a1c2b8d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl inspect 3f9a1c2b8d | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl pods\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>systemctl status containerd\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Isolation can also be strengthened with alternative low-level runtimes such as gVisor or Kata Containers, which add a user-space kernel or a lightweight virtual machine between the workload and the host. They trade some performance for stronger separation and are chosen with a \u003ccode>RuntimeClass\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-docker-command-not-found-on-the-node\">Incident: docker command not found on the node\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A Pod is stuck in \u003ccode>ImagePullBackOff\u003c/code>. The on-call engineer connects to the node, runs \u003ccode>docker pull\u003c/code> to test the registry, and gets \u003ccode>command not found\u003c/code>. They conclude the node is broken and drain it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The node is fine. It uses containerd, so there is no Docker. The kubelet&#39;s view of the problem is available with the CRI tool:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sudo crictl pull registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\nkubectl get secret regcred -n shop -o jsonpath='{.type}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sudo\u003c/span> crictl pull registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FATA[0000] pulling image: ... 401 Unauthorized\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get secret regcred -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.type}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The real cause is an expired registry credential. Refresh the image pull secret, and teach the team that \u003ccode>crictl\u003c/code> replaces \u003ccode>docker\u003c/code> on nodes, and that image pull errors are about names, authentication and network, not the node.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming every node has the Docker daemon\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running containers as root on the host when rootless is possible\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Confusing the runtime spec with the image spec\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mounting the Docker socket into containers, which is equivalent to root on the host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a sandbox runtime without checking it supports the features you need\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-layers","text":"The layers"},{"id":"the-open-container-initiative","text":"The Open Container Initiative"},{"id":"podman","text":"Podman"},{"id":"runtimes-under-kubernetes","text":"Runtimes under Kubernetes"},{"id":"incident-docker-command-not-found-on-the-node","text":"Incident: docker command not found on the node"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Open Container Initiative","u":"https://opencontainers.org/about/overview/"},{"t":"Podman documentation","u":"https://docs.podman.io/en/latest/"},{"t":"containerd documentation","u":"https://containerd.io/docs/"}],"text":"Docker made containers popular, but Docker is not the only piece of software involved. Kubernetes does not need the Docker daemon at all. Knowing the layers helps you debug nodes, choose tools and read documentation that mentions containerd, runc or CRI. What you will learn The layers: CLI, engine, high-level runtime and low-level runtime What the OCI image and runtime specifications guarantee Using Podman as a daemonless, rootless alternative Inspecting containers on a Kubernetes node with crictl The layers When you run docker run , several programs cooperate: Layer Example Job CLI docker , podman , nerdctl Takes your command Engine / API dockerd Builds images, manages networks and volumes High-level runtime containerd , CRI-O Pulls images, manages container lifecycle Low-level runtime runc , crun Creates the Linux namespaces and cgroups and starts the process At the bottom a container is just a Linux process started with namespaces (an isolated view of processes, network, mounts and users) and cgroups (limits on CPU and memory). runc is the reference tool that does exactly that. The Open Container Initiative The OCI publishes two specifications. The image spec defines how an image is laid out: a manifest, a configuration and a stack of layer tarballs. The runtime spec defines how a runtime turns an unpacked image into a running process. Because of these standards, an image built with Docker runs on containerd, CRI-O or Podman, and Kubernetes can swap its runtime without changing your images. $ docker save shop-api:1.8.2 -o shop-api.tar $ tar -tf shop-api.tar | head $ docker history shop-api:1.8.2 $ docker inspect shop-api:1.8.2 --format &#x27;{{.Config.Entrypoint}} {{.Config.Cmd}}&#x27; Podman Podman runs containers without a long-running daemon, and can do so as a normal user ( rootless ). Its commands are deliberately compatible with Docker&#39;s. $ podman run -- rm -it docker.io/library/alpine:3.20 uname -a $ podman build -t shop-api:dev . $ podman ps $ podman generate systemd --new --name shop-api > shop-api.service $ podman pod create --name shop -p 8080:80 $ podman run -d --pod shop --name web docker.io/library/nginx:1.27 In rootless mode, \"root\" inside the container maps to your unprivileged user on the host. If an attacker escapes the container they land as an unprivileged user, which greatly limits damage. Podman can also run several containers in a pod that shares a network namespace, the same idea as a Kubernetes Pod, and can export or read Kubernetes YAML with podman generate kube and podman play kube . Runtimes under Kubernetes Modern Kubernetes talks to a runtime through the Container Runtime Interface (CRI) . Most clusters use containerd or CRI-O. On a node there may be no docker command, so use crictl to see what the kubelet sees. $ sudo crictl ps $ sudo crictl images $ sudo crictl logs 3f9a1c2b8d $ sudo crictl inspect 3f9a1c2b8d | head -30 $ sudo crictl pods $ systemctl status containerd Isolation can also be strengthened with alternative low-level runtimes such as gVisor or Kata Containers, which add a user-space kernel or a lightweight virtual machine between the workload and the host. They trade some performance for stronger separation and are chosen with a RuntimeClass . Incident: docker command not found on the node Incident A Pod is stuck in ImagePullBackOff . The on-call engineer connects to the node, runs docker pull to test the registry, and gets command not found . They conclude the node is broken and drain it. The node is fine. It uses containerd, so there is no Docker. The kubelet&#39;s view of the problem is available with the CRI tool: $ sudo crictl pull registry.example.com/team/shop-api:1.8.2 FATA[0000] pulling image: ... 401 Unauthorized $ kubectl get secret regcred -n shop -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.type}&#x27; kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson The real cause is an expired registry credential. Refresh the image pull secret, and teach the team that crictl replaces docker on nodes, and that image pull"},"docker/security-and-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>A container keeps restarting. Another one passed review and then showed up in a vulnerability report. This lesson covers both sides of operating containers: reducing what an attacker can do, and finding out quickly what went wrong when something stops.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A short hardening checklist for running containers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What belongs in an image and what does not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to read container exit codes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to find out whether a container was killed for memory\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hardening-checklist\">Hardening checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Practice\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>How\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Run as a non-root user\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>USER nonroot\u003c/code> in the Dockerfile, or \u003ccode>--user 10001\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Read-only root filesystem\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--read-only\u003c/code>, with \u003ccode>--tmpfs /tmp\u003c/code> for scratch space\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Drop Linux capabilities\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--cap-drop ALL\u003c/code>, then add only what you need\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Block privilege escalation\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--security-opt no-new-privileges\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Limit resources\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>--memory 512m --cpus 1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Keep secrets out of images\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Use runtime secrets, never \u003ccode>COPY\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>ENV\u003c/code> secrets\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pin and scan images\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Use fixed tags or digests and scan for known vulnerabilities\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run -d --name api \\\n  --user 10001:10001 \\\n  --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\\n  --cap-drop ALL \\\n  --security-opt no-new-privileges \\\n  --memory 512m --cpus 1 \\\n  registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker run -d --name api \\\n  --user 10001:10001 \\\n  --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\\n  --cap-drop ALL \\\n  --security-opt no-new-privileges \\\n  --memory 512m --cpus 1 \\\n  registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Avoid \u003ccode>--privileged\u003c/code> and mounting \u003ccode>/var/run/docker.sock\u003c/code> into containers. Either gives the container nearly full control of the host, because anyone who can talk to the Docker socket can start a privileged container.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Every package in an image is something to patch. Choose a small base, rebuild regularly for security updates, and scan in your pipeline:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--exit-code 1\u003c/code> makes the command fail when it finds serious issues, which turns the scan into a pipeline gate. Remember that a scan shows \u003cem>known\u003c/em> issues in the packages it can identify. It does not replace least privilege.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-did-my-container-stop\">Why did my container stop?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with the state Docker recorded:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker ps -a --filter name=api\ndocker inspect api --format 'exit={{.State.ExitCode}} oom={{.State.OOMKilled}} restarts={{.RestartCount}}'\ndocker logs --tail 20 api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker ps -a --filter name=api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CONTAINER ID   IMAGE          STATUS                       NAMES\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">8d9e0a1b2c3d   shop-api:1.8   Exited (137) 3 minutes ago   api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect api --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;exit={{.State.ExitCode}} oom={{.State.OOMKilled}} restarts={{.RestartCount}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">exit=137 oom=true restarts=4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker logs --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> 20 api\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The exit code tells you a lot. Codes above 128 mean the process died from a signal, calculated as \u003ccode>128 + signal number\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Exit code\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>0\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Finished normally\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The application reported an error; read the logs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>125\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Docker itself failed to run the command\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>126\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Command found but not executable\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>127\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Command not found, often a wrong path or missing binary in the image\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>137\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Killed by \u003ccode>SIGKILL\u003c/code> (9), commonly out of memory or \u003ccode>docker kill\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>139\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Segmentation fault (11)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>143\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stopped by \u003ccode>SIGTERM\u003c/code> (15), a normal stop\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For a container that will not start or exits instantly, run it interactively with a different entrypoint to explore:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run --rm -it --entrypoint sh registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --entrypoint sh registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the image has no shell (distroless), use \u003ccode>docker logs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>docker inspect\u003c/code>, or Docker&#39;s debug tooling to attach a shell from another image sharing the same namespaces. Do not add a shell to the production image just for convenience.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-container-is-killed-every-few-hours\">Incident: the container is killed every few hours\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>api\u003c/code> restarts about every four hours with \u003ccode>Exited (137)\u003c/code>. The logs show nothing unusual before the restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The output above already contains the answer: \u003ccode>exit=137 oom=true\u003c/code>. The kernel killed the process for exceeding the container&#39;s memory limit, so the application had no chance to log anything. To see the trend instead of guessing:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker stats --no-stream api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker stats --no-stream api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CONTAINER ID   NAME   CPU %   MEM USAGE / LIMIT   MEM %    NET I/O\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">8d9e0a1b2c3d   api    12.4%   498MiB / 512MiB     97.3%    1.2GB / 800MB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Memory sits at 97% of the 512 MiB limit. Two honest options exist. If the service legitimately needs more memory under load, raise the limit after checking peak usage. If memory grows steadily until it dies, you have a leak: capture a heap profile or trend memory in your metrics, fix the code, and keep the limit as a safety net. Raising the limit repeatedly without watching the trend only delays the next kill. Also set a restart policy such as \u003ccode>--restart unless-stopped\u003c/code> so a crash does not become an outage, while still alerting on restart counts so you notice.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running with \u003ccode>--privileged\u003c/code> or mounting the Docker socket\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Adding secrets with \u003ccode>ENV\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>COPY\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restarting a crashing container repeatedly without reading the exit code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting no memory limit and letting one container starve the host\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"hardening-checklist","text":"Hardening checklist"},{"id":"why-did-my-container-stop","text":"Why did my container stop?"},{"id":"incident-the-container-is-killed-every-few-hours","text":"Incident: the container is killed every few hours"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker Engine security","u":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/"},{"t":"Trivy documentation","u":"https://trivy.dev/latest/docs/"},{"t":"Docker: build best practices","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/"}],"text":"A container keeps restarting. Another one passed review and then showed up in a vulnerability report. This lesson covers both sides of operating containers: reducing what an attacker can do, and finding out quickly what went wrong when something stops. What you will learn A short hardening checklist for running containers What belongs in an image and what does not How to read container exit codes How to find out whether a container was killed for memory Hardening checklist Practice How Run as a non-root user USER nonroot in the Dockerfile, or --user 10001 Read-only root filesystem --read-only , with --tmpfs /tmp for scratch space Drop Linux capabilities --cap-drop ALL , then add only what you need Block privilege escalation --security-opt no-new-privileges Limit resources --memory 512m --cpus 1 Keep secrets out of images Use runtime secrets, never COPY or ENV secrets Pin and scan images Use fixed tags or digests and scan for known vulnerabilities docker run -d --name api \\ --user 10001:10001 \\ --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\ --cap-drop ALL \\ --security-opt no-new-privileges \\ --memory 512m --cpus 1 \\ registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2 Warning Avoid --privileged and mounting /var/run/docker.sock into containers. Either gives the container nearly full control of the host, because anyone who can talk to the Docker socket can start a privileged container. Every package in an image is something to patch. Choose a small base, rebuild regularly for security updates, and scan in your pipeline: $ trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2 --exit-code 1 makes the command fail when it finds serious issues, which turns the scan into a pipeline gate. Remember that a scan shows known issues in the packages it can identify. It does not replace least privilege. Why did my container stop? Start with the state Docker recorded: $ docker ps -a --filter name=api CONTAINER ID IMAGE STATUS NAMES 8d9e0a1b2c3d shop-api:1.8 Exited (137) 3 minutes ago api $ docker inspect api --format &#x27;exit={{.State.ExitCode}} oom={{.State.OOMKilled}} restarts={{.RestartCount}}&#x27; exit=137 oom=true restarts=4 $ docker logs -- tail 20 api The exit code tells you a lot. Codes above 128 mean the process died from a signal, calculated as 128 + signal number . Exit code Meaning 0 Finished normally 1 The application reported an error; read the logs 125 Docker itself failed to run the command 126 Command found but not executable 127 Command not found, often a wrong path or missing binary in the image 137 Killed by SIGKILL (9), commonly out of memory or docker kill 139 Segmentation fault (11) 143 Stopped by SIGTERM (15), a normal stop For a container that will not start or exits instantly, run it interactively with a different entrypoint to explore: docker run -- rm -it --entrypoint sh registry.example.com/shop-api:1.8.2 If the image has no shell (distroless), use docker logs , docker inspect , or Docker&#39;s debug tooling to attach a shell from another image sharing the same namespaces. Do not add a shell to the production image just for convenience. Incident: the container is killed every few hours Incident api restarts about every four hours with Exited (137) . The logs show nothing unusual before the restart. The output above already contains the answer: exit=137 oom=true . The kernel killed the process for exceeding the container&#39;s memory limit, so the application had no chance to log anything. To see the trend instead of guessing: $ docker stats --no-stream api CONTAINER ID NAME CPU % MEM USAGE / LIMIT MEM % NET I/O 8d9e0a1b2c3d api 12.4% 498MiB / 512MiB 97.3% 1.2GB / 800MB Memory sits at 97% of the 512 MiB limit. Two honest options exist. If the service legitimately needs more memory under load, raise the limit after checking peak usage. If memory grows steadily until it dies, you have a leak: capture a heap profile or trend memory in your metrics, fix the code, and keep the limit as a safety net. Raising the limit rep"},"docker/buildkit-caching-and-multi-arch":{"html":"\u003cp>A slow image build slows every developer and every pipeline run. A careless one leaks secrets into layers or produces an image that cannot run on the machine where it is deployed. BuildKit, the modern build engine, gives you precise control over caching, secrets and target platforms.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How layer caching works and how to order instructions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache mounts for package managers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build secrets and SSH forwarding without leaking into layers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing cache between pipeline runs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building for amd64 and arm64 with \u003ccode>buildx\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-the-cache-works\">How the cache works\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each instruction in a Dockerfile creates a layer. If the instruction and everything before it are unchanged, the layer comes from cache. As soon as one layer changes, all following layers are rebuilt. So order instructions from least to most frequently changing:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM python:3.12-slim AS build\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .                      # changes rarely\nRUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/pip \\\n    pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\nCOPY . .                                      # changes on every commit\nRUN python -m compileall .\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> python:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.12\u003c/span>-slim AS build\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /app\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> requirements.txt .                      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># changes rarely\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> --mount=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=cache,target=/root/.cache/pip \\\n    pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .                                      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># changes on every commit\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> python -m compileall .\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Copying the dependency list first means a code change does not reinstall the dependencies. A \u003ccode>.dockerignore\u003c/code> file keeps unrelated changes (test output, local files) from invalidating the \u003ccode>COPY . .\u003c/code> layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cache-mounts\">Cache mounts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>RUN --mount=type=cache\u003c/code> keeps a directory (pip, npm, apt, Go build cache) between builds without storing it in the image. Builds become much faster, and the image stays small.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets\">Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never use \u003ccode>ARG\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>ENV\u003c/code> for a secret, or \u003ccode>COPY\u003c/code> a credentials file: they stay in image layers and history. Use a build secret, which is mounted only during one \u003ccode>RUN\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"FROM node:20-slim\nRUN --mount=type=secret,id=npmrc,target=/root/.npmrc npm ci\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> node:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>-slim\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> --mount=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=secret,\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>=npmrc,target=/root/.npmrc npm ci\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker build --secret id=npmrc,src=$HOME/.npmrc -t shop:1.0 .\ndocker history --no-trunc shop:1.0 | grep -i npmrc || echo &quot;not in history&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker build --secret \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>=npmrc,src=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$HOME\u003c/span>/.npmrc -t shop:1.0 .\ndocker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> --no-trunc shop:1.0 | grep -i npmrc || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;not in history&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For private repositories use \u003ccode>--mount=type=ssh\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>--ssh default\u003c/code> so the key never enters the image.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cache-in-pipelines\">Cache in pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pipeline runners start clean, so local layer cache is empty. Export and import the cache:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker buildx build \\\n  --cache-from type=registry,ref=registry.example.com/shop:buildcache \\\n  --cache-to   type=registry,ref=registry.example.com/shop:buildcache,mode=max \\\n  -t registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2 --push .\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker buildx build \\\n  --cache-from \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=registry,ref=registry.example.com/shop:buildcache \\\n  --cache-to   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>=registry,ref=registry.example.com/shop:buildcache,mode=max \\\n  -t registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2 --push .\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multi-architecture-images\">Multi-architecture images\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Teams now run on both amd64 servers and arm64 machines (developer laptops, cloud processors that cost less). An image built for one fails on the other with &quot;exec format error&quot;. Build a manifest list covering both:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker buildx create --use\ndocker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 -t registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2 --push .\ndocker buildx imagetools inspect registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2   # shows each platform\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker buildx create --use\ndocker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 -t registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2 --push .\ndocker buildx imagetools inspect registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># shows each platform\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Compile inside the build for the target (for example \u003ccode>GOARCH=$TARGETARCH\u003c/code>), and test the arm64 image, not only that it builds. Emulation with QEMU works but is slow, so use native builders when you can.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You move `COPY . .` above `RUN pip install -r requirements.txt`. What happens to build times?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Every code change invalidates the cache before the install step, so dependencies are reinstalled on every build.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Put the files that change least first.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-token-in-the-layer\">Incident: the token in the layer\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A security scan finds a package registry token in a public image, though the Dockerfile&#39;s last line removes the file.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker history --no-trunc shop:0.9 | grep -i NPM_TOKEN\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> --no-trunc shop:0.9 | grep -i NPM_TOKEN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> ARG NPM_TOKEN=npm_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The token was passed as a build argument and a \u003ccode>RUN rm\u003c/code> in a later layer does not remove earlier layers. The team revoked the token, rebuilt with a build secret, scanned the history in CI, and added a rule against \u003ccode>ARG\u003c/code> names that contain TOKEN or KEY.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>COPY . .\u003c/code> before dependency installation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Secrets in \u003ccode>ARG\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ENV\u003c/code> or files copied into layers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No \u003ccode>.dockerignore\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Publishing a single-architecture image to mixed fleets\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-the-cache-works","text":"How the cache works"},{"id":"cache-mounts","text":"Cache mounts"},{"id":"secrets","text":"Secrets"},{"id":"cache-in-pipelines","text":"Cache in pipelines"},{"id":"multi-architecture-images","text":"Multi-architecture images"},{"id":"incident-the-token-in-the-layer","text":"Incident: the token in the layer"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker docs: Build cache","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/cache/"},{"t":"Docker docs: Multi-platform builds","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-platform/"}],"text":"A slow image build slows every developer and every pipeline run. A careless one leaks secrets into layers or produces an image that cannot run on the machine where it is deployed. BuildKit, the modern build engine, gives you precise control over caching, secrets and target platforms. What you will learn How layer caching works and how to order instructions Cache mounts for package managers Build secrets and SSH forwarding without leaking into layers Sharing cache between pipeline runs Building for amd64 and arm64 with buildx How the cache works Each instruction in a Dockerfile creates a layer. If the instruction and everything before it are unchanged, the layer comes from cache. As soon as one layer changes, all following layers are rebuilt. So order instructions from least to most frequently changing: # syntax=docker/dockerfile:1 FROM python: 3.12 -slim AS build WORKDIR /app COPY requirements.txt . # changes rarely RUN --mount= type =cache,target=/root/.cache/pip \\ pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt COPY . . # changes on every commit RUN python -m compileall . Copying the dependency list first means a code change does not reinstall the dependencies. A .dockerignore file keeps unrelated changes (test output, local files) from invalidating the COPY . . layer. Cache mounts RUN --mount=type=cache keeps a directory (pip, npm, apt, Go build cache) between builds without storing it in the image. Builds become much faster, and the image stays small. Secrets Never use ARG or ENV for a secret, or COPY a credentials file: they stay in image layers and history. Use a build secret, which is mounted only during one RUN : FROM node: 20 -slim RUN --mount= type =secret, id =npmrc,target=/root/.npmrc npm ci docker build --secret id =npmrc,src= $HOME /.npmrc -t shop:1.0 . docker history --no-trunc shop:1.0 | grep -i npmrc || echo \"not in history\" For private repositories use --mount=type=ssh and --ssh default so the key never enters the image. Cache in pipelines Pipeline runners start clean, so local layer cache is empty. Export and import the cache: docker buildx build \\ --cache-from type =registry,ref=registry.example.com/shop:buildcache \\ --cache-to type =registry,ref=registry.example.com/shop:buildcache,mode=max \\ -t registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2 --push . Multi-architecture images Teams now run on both amd64 servers and arm64 machines (developer laptops, cloud processors that cost less). An image built for one fails on the other with \"exec format error\". Build a manifest list covering both: docker buildx create --use docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 -t registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2 --push . docker buildx imagetools inspect registry.example.com/shop:1.4.2 # shows each platform Compile inside the build for the target (for example GOARCH=$TARGETARCH ), and test the arm64 image, not only that it builds. Emulation with QEMU works but is slow, so use native builders when you can. Predict first. You move `COPY . .` above `RUN pip install -r requirements.txt`. What happens to build times? Your guess Answer: Every code change invalidates the cache before the install step, so dependencies are reinstalled on every build. Put the files that change least first. Incident: the token in the layer Incident A security scan finds a package registry token in a public image, though the Dockerfile&#39;s last line removes the file. $ docker history --no-trunc shop:0.9 | grep -i NPM_TOKEN ARG NPM_TOKEN=npm_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx The token was passed as a build argument and a RUN rm in a later layer does not remove earlier layers. The team revoked the token, rebuilt with a build secret, scanned the history in CI, and added a rule against ARG names that contain TOKEN or KEY. Common mistakes COPY . . before dependency installation Secrets in ARG , ENV or files copied into layers No .dockerignore Publishing a single-architecture image to mixed fleets"},"docker/container-logging-and-resource-limits":{"html":"\u003cp>Containers run for months and fail at awkward times. The three things that decide whether you can cope are: whether you can see what happened (logs), whether the platform can tell if the app is healthy (health checks), and whether one container can harm its neighbours (limits).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why containers log to standard output and how logging drivers work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preventing logs from filling the disk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks that tell the truth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CPU, memory, process and file limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restart policies and their traps\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logging\">Logging\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A container&#39;s main process should write logs to standard output and standard error, not to files inside the container. The runtime captures them, and \u003ccode>docker logs\u003c/code> or the platform collects them. Files inside the container are lost with the container and fill its writable layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>By default the \u003ccode>json-file\u003c/code> driver stores logs without a size limit, so a chatty service can fill the host&#39;s disk. Set rotation:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{ &quot;log-driver&quot;: &quot;json-file&quot;, &quot;log-opts&quot;: { &quot;max-size&quot;: &quot;10m&quot;, &quot;max-file&quot;: &quot;3&quot; } }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;log-driver&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;json-file&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;log-opts&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;max-size&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10m&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;max-file&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;3&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>in \u003ccode>/etc/docker/daemon.json\u003c/code>, or per container with \u003ccode>--log-opt max-size=10m --log-opt max-file=3\u003c/code>. For central logging, use a driver or an agent that ships logs to your log system. Write structured (JSON) logs with a request id so they can be searched.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-checks\">Health checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"FROM alpine:3.20\nHEALTHCHECK --interval=15s --timeout=3s --start-period=30s --retries=3 \\\n  CMD wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1:8080/health || exit 1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> alpine:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3.20\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">HEALTHCHECK\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> --interval=15s --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span>=3s --start-period=30s --retries=3 \\\n  CMD wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1:8080/health || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A good health endpoint checks that the app can do its job (for example, reach its database), but not so much that a slow dependency makes every instance unhealthy at once. Separate readiness (can it take traffic) from liveness (should it be restarted). A \u003ccode>start-period\u003c/code> stops slow-starting apps from being killed before they are ready.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits\">Limits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run -d --name shop \\\n  --memory 512m --memory-swap 512m \\\n  --cpus 1.0 \\\n  --pids-limit 200 \\\n  --ulimit nofile=4096:4096 \\\n  --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\\n  shop:1.4.2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker run -d --name shop \\\n  --memory 512m --memory-swap 512m \\\n  --cpus 1.0 \\\n  --pids-limit 200 \\\n  --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ulimit\u003c/span> nofile=4096:4096 \\\n  --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\\n  shop:1.4.2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--memory\u003c/code> is a hard limit: going over it ends in an OOM kill (exit code 137). Set \u003ccode>--memory-swap\u003c/code> equal to it to disable swap use.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--cpus\u003c/code> throttles; it does not kill. Throttling appears as latency.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--pids-limit\u003c/code> stops fork bombs and thread leaks.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--ulimit nofile\u003c/code> limits open files.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>--read-only\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>--tmpfs\u003c/code> limits what an attacker or a bug can write.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Choose limits from measured use: run under realistic load, look at \u003ccode>docker stats\u003c/code>, and add headroom. Limits that are too tight cause restarts; none at all lets one container starve the host.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"restart-policies\">Restart policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--restart unless-stopped\u003c/code> restarts a container that exits. This helps with rare crashes but hides a crash loop. Always alert on restart counts, and pair restarts with logs that show why the process exited.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A container with --memory 256m is repeatedly restarted. docker inspect shows OOMKilled true. Name two fixes.\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Raise the limit after measuring real use, or reduce the application's memory use (heap size, cache size, worker count).\u003c/p>\u003cp>A restart policy only hides the problem. Fix the limit or the memory use, and alert on memory approaching the limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-disk-that-filled-with-logs\">Incident: the disk that filled with logs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A host&#39;s disk reaches 100 percent overnight and all containers on it stop working.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"du -sh /var/lib/docker/containers/*/*-json.log | sort -h | tail -2\ndocker logs --tail 3 api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> -sh /var/lib/docker/containers/*/*-json.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -h | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">48G  /var/lib/docker/containers/9f1c.../9f1c...-json.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker logs --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> 3 api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">DEBUG cache miss key=...\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A debug log level had been left on, and the default log driver had no size limit. The team set rotation in the daemon configuration, moved to structured logs sent to the central system, and added a disk usage alert at 80 percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Logging to files inside containers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No log rotation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Health checks that check nothing, or everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Restart policies without restart alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"logging","text":"Logging"},{"id":"health-checks","text":"Health checks"},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits"},{"id":"restart-policies","text":"Restart policies"},{"id":"incident-the-disk-that-filled-with-logs","text":"Incident: the disk that filled with logs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker docs: Logging drivers","u":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/logging/configure/"},{"t":"Docker docs: Resource constraints","u":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/containers/resource_constraints/"}],"text":"Containers run for months and fail at awkward times. The three things that decide whether you can cope are: whether you can see what happened (logs), whether the platform can tell if the app is healthy (health checks), and whether one container can harm its neighbours (limits). What you will learn Why containers log to standard output and how logging drivers work Preventing logs from filling the disk Health checks that tell the truth CPU, memory, process and file limits Restart policies and their traps Logging A container&#39;s main process should write logs to standard output and standard error, not to files inside the container. The runtime captures them, and docker logs or the platform collects them. Files inside the container are lost with the container and fill its writable layer. By default the json-file driver stores logs without a size limit, so a chatty service can fill the host&#39;s disk. Set rotation: { \"log-driver\" : \"json-file\" , \"log-opts\" : { \"max-size\" : \"10m\" , \"max-file\" : \"3\" } } in /etc/docker/daemon.json , or per container with --log-opt max-size=10m --log-opt max-file=3 . For central logging, use a driver or an agent that ships logs to your log system. Write structured (JSON) logs with a request id so they can be searched. Health checks FROM alpine: 3.20 HEALTHCHECK --interval=15s -- timeout =3s --start-period=30s --retries=3 \\ CMD wget -qO- http://127.0.0.1:8080/health || exit 1 A good health endpoint checks that the app can do its job (for example, reach its database), but not so much that a slow dependency makes every instance unhealthy at once. Separate readiness (can it take traffic) from liveness (should it be restarted). A start-period stops slow-starting apps from being killed before they are ready. Limits docker run -d --name shop \\ --memory 512m --memory-swap 512m \\ --cpus 1.0 \\ --pids-limit 200 \\ -- ulimit nofile=4096:4096 \\ --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\ shop:1.4.2 --memory is a hard limit: going over it ends in an OOM kill (exit code 137). Set --memory-swap equal to it to disable swap use. --cpus throttles; it does not kill. Throttling appears as latency. --pids-limit stops fork bombs and thread leaks. --ulimit nofile limits open files. --read-only with --tmpfs limits what an attacker or a bug can write. Choose limits from measured use: run under realistic load, look at docker stats , and add headroom. Limits that are too tight cause restarts; none at all lets one container starve the host. Restart policies --restart unless-stopped restarts a container that exits. This helps with rare crashes but hides a crash loop. Always alert on restart counts, and pair restarts with logs that show why the process exited. Predict first. A container with --memory 256m is repeatedly restarted. docker inspect shows OOMKilled true. Name two fixes. Your guess Answer: Raise the limit after measuring real use, or reduce the application's memory use (heap size, cache size, worker count). A restart policy only hides the problem. Fix the limit or the memory use, and alert on memory approaching the limit. Incident: the disk that filled with logs Incident A host&#39;s disk reaches 100 percent overnight and all containers on it stop working. $ du -sh /var/lib/docker/containers/*/*-json.log | sort -h | tail -2 48G /var/lib/docker/containers/9f1c.../9f1c...-json.log $ docker logs -- tail 3 api DEBUG cache miss key=... A debug log level had been left on, and the default log driver had no size limit. The team set rotation in the daemon configuration, moved to structured logs sent to the central system, and added a disk usage alert at 80 percent. Common mistakes Logging to files inside containers No log rotation Health checks that check nothing, or everything Restart policies without restart alerts"},"docker/slim-images-and-faster-builds":{"html":"\u003cp>An image of 1.4 GB takes minutes to pull on every new node, contains compilers an attacker could use, and shows hundreds of vulnerabilities in a scan. Most of that weight is not your application. Small images start faster, cost less to store and give an attacker less to work with.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a base image on purpose: full, slim, alpine or distroless\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use multi-stage builds to leave build tools behind\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep layers small and avoid hidden bulk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect an image to see where the size comes from\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pick-the-base-on-purpose\">Pick the base on purpose\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The base image is usually most of the size. Typical choices:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Base\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Size\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>python:3.12\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 1 GB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Compilers and tools, good for building\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>python:3.12-slim\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 150 MB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Debian without most extras, a safe default\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>python:3.12-alpine\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>about 50 MB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Uses musl, which can break some compiled packages\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>distroless\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>20 to 60 MB\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No shell or package manager, hardest to attack\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Alpine is small but uses a different C library, and some Python wheels then have to be built from source. Slim images are the practical middle. Distroless images are excellent for production, but without a shell you debug with an ephemeral debug container or a separate debug tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multi-stage-builds\">Multi-stage builds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Build tools are needed to create the program, not to run it. A multi-stage Dockerfile builds in one stage and copies only the result into a small final stage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"dockerfile\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"FROM golang:1.22 AS build\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download\nCOPY . .\nRUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/app ./cmd/app\n\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12\nCOPY --from=build /out/app /app\nUSER nonroot\nENTRYPOINT [&quot;/app&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> golang:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.22\u003c/span> AS build\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">WORKDIR\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> /src\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> go.mod go.sum ./\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> go mod download\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> . .\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">RUN\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/app ./cmd/app\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM\u003c/span> gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">COPY\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> --from=build /out/app /app\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">USER\u003c/span> nonroot\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">ENTRYPOINT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"language-bash\"> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/app&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The final image holds one static binary. The compiler, the source code and the module cache stay in the first stage and are not part of what you ship.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layers-and-what-hides-in-them\">Layers and what hides in them\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every instruction can add a layer, and a file deleted in a later layer still takes space in the earlier one. Clean up in the same instruction that creates the mess.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"RUN apt-get update \\\n &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl ca-certificates \\\n &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">RUN apt-get update \\\n &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl ca-certificates \\\n &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use a \u003ccode>.dockerignore\u003c/code> file so the build context does not include \u003ccode>node_modules\u003c/code>, test data, local virtual environments or secrets. Copy the files that change least first (dependency lists), so the cache is reused when only source code changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"see-where-the-size-comes-from\">See where the size comes from\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Measure before you optimize.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker images shop\ndocker history shop:1.0 | head\ndocker run --rm -it wagoodman/dive shop:1.0\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker images shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">REPOSITORY   TAG    SIZE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop         1.0    1.38GB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> shop:1.0 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it wagoodman/dive shop:1.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>docker history\u003c/code> lists layers with their sizes, and tools such as \u003ccode>dive\u003c/code> show which files each layer added. A scan with Trivy or Grype on the slim image typically reports far fewer packages, which means fewer alerts to triage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You copy a 600 MB archive into an image in one RUN and delete it in the next. Is the image smaller?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. The first layer still contains the archive. Download, use and delete it in a single RUN, or use a multi-stage build.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Layers are additive; a later delete only hides the file.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-1-4-gb-image-on-every-node\">Incident: A 1.4 GB image on every node\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Scale-out events took ten minutes because each new node pulled the application image from the registry first.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker history shop:1.0 --format &quot;{{.Size}}\\t{{.CreatedBy}}&quot; | head -4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> shop:1.0 --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{{.Size}}\\t{{.CreatedBy}}&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">610MB   RUN /bin/sh -c apt-get install -y build-essential\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">480MB   COPY . /app\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">210MB   RUN pip install -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The image carried compilers and the whole repository, including test data and a local virtual environment. A multi-stage build, a slim base, and a \u003ccode>.dockerignore\u003c/code> brought it down to 170 MB. Pulls dropped from minutes to seconds, and the vulnerability scan went from over 400 findings to 31.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the full language image as the final image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting files in a later layer and expecting the size to drop\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copying the whole project, including secrets and caches, into the image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing Alpine for Python and then fighting native build errors\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"pick-the-base-on-purpose","text":"Pick the base on purpose"},{"id":"multi-stage-builds","text":"Multi-stage builds"},{"id":"layers-and-what-hides-in-them","text":"Layers and what hides in them"},{"id":"see-where-the-size-comes-from","text":"See where the size comes from"},{"id":"incident-a-1-4-gb-image-on-every-node","text":"Incident: A 1.4 GB image on every node"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Dockerfile best practices","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/"},{"t":"Multi-stage builds","u":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/"}],"text":"An image of 1.4 GB takes minutes to pull on every new node, contains compilers an attacker could use, and shows hundreds of vulnerabilities in a scan. Most of that weight is not your application. Small images start faster, cost less to store and give an attacker less to work with. What you will learn Choose a base image on purpose: full, slim, alpine or distroless Use multi-stage builds to leave build tools behind Keep layers small and avoid hidden bulk Inspect an image to see where the size comes from Pick the base on purpose The base image is usually most of the size. Typical choices: Base Size Notes python:3.12 about 1 GB Compilers and tools, good for building python:3.12-slim about 150 MB Debian without most extras, a safe default python:3.12-alpine about 50 MB Uses musl, which can break some compiled packages distroless 20 to 60 MB No shell or package manager, hardest to attack Alpine is small but uses a different C library, and some Python wheels then have to be built from source. Slim images are the practical middle. Distroless images are excellent for production, but without a shell you debug with an ephemeral debug container or a separate debug tag. Multi-stage builds Build tools are needed to create the program, not to run it. A multi-stage Dockerfile builds in one stage and copies only the result into a small final stage. FROM golang: 1.22 AS build WORKDIR /src COPY go.mod go.sum ./ RUN go mod download COPY . . RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o /out/app ./cmd/app FROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12 COPY --from=build /out/app /app USER nonroot ENTRYPOINT [ \"/app\" ] The final image holds one static binary. The compiler, the source code and the module cache stay in the first stage and are not part of what you ship. Layers and what hides in them Every instruction can add a layer, and a file deleted in a later layer still takes space in the earlier one. Clean up in the same instruction that creates the mess. RUN apt-get update \\ && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl ca-certificates \\ && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* Use a .dockerignore file so the build context does not include node_modules , test data, local virtual environments or secrets. Copy the files that change least first (dependency lists), so the cache is reused when only source code changes. See where the size comes from Measure before you optimize. $ docker images shop REPOSITORY TAG SIZE shop 1.0 1.38GB $ docker history shop:1.0 | head $ docker run -- rm -it wagoodman/dive shop:1.0 docker history lists layers with their sizes, and tools such as dive show which files each layer added. A scan with Trivy or Grype on the slim image typically reports far fewer packages, which means fewer alerts to triage. Predict first. You copy a 600 MB archive into an image in one RUN and delete it in the next. Is the image smaller? Your guess Answer: No. The first layer still contains the archive. Download, use and delete it in a single RUN, or use a multi-stage build. Layers are additive; a later delete only hides the file. Incident: A 1.4 GB image on every node Incident Scale-out events took ten minutes because each new node pulled the application image from the registry first. $ docker history shop:1.0 --format \"{{.Size}}\\t{{.CreatedBy}}\" | head -4 610MB RUN /bin/sh -c apt-get install -y build-essential 480MB COPY . /app 210MB RUN pip install -r requirements.txt The image carried compilers and the whole repository, including test data and a local virtual environment. A multi-stage build, a slim base, and a .dockerignore brought it down to 170 MB. Pulls dropped from minutes to seconds, and the vulnerability scan went from over 400 findings to 31. Common mistakes Using the full language image as the final image Deleting files in a later layer and expecting the size to drop Copying the whole project, including secrets and caches, into the image Choosing Alpine for Python and then fighting native build errors"},"docker/running-containers-in-production":{"html":"\u003cp>A container that works on a laptop can still be a poor citizen in production. It runs as root, it ignores the stop signal and is killed after ten seconds mid-request, and it has no limits, so one bug takes the host down. A short checklist of run-time settings turns a working container into a dependable one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Run as a non-root user with a read-only filesystem and dropped capabilities\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handle SIGTERM for graceful shutdown\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set resource limits, health checks and a restart policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Log to standard output and keep data in volumes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reduce-what-a-container-may-do\">Reduce what a container may do\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Containers share the host kernel, so you narrow their rights. Four settings do most of the work:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run -d --name shop \\\n    --user 10001:10001 \\\n    --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\\n    --cap-drop ALL \\\n    --security-opt no-new-privileges \\\n    shop:1.4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run -d --name shop \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --user 10001:10001 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --cap-drop ALL \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --security-opt no-new-privileges \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    shop:1.4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>--user\u003c/code> avoids root inside the container, \u003ccode>--read-only\u003c/code> stops an attacker from changing the program, \u003ccode>--tmpfs /tmp\u003c/code> provides the one writable place most programs need, and \u003ccode>--cap-drop ALL\u003c/code> removes Linux capabilities that a web service never uses. Add back only what you must, for example \u003ccode>--cap-add NET_BIND_SERVICE\u003c/code> to bind a port below 1024.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stop-gracefully\">Stop gracefully\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>docker stop\u003c/code> sends SIGTERM and waits ten seconds before SIGKILL. A service must catch SIGTERM, stop accepting new work, finish current requests and exit. In a Dockerfile, use the exec form so the program is process 1 and receives the signal:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ENTRYPOINT [&quot;/app/server&quot;]        # good: the server gets SIGTERM\n# ENTRYPOINT /app/server           # shell form: a shell is PID 1 and may swallow it\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">ENTRYPOINT [&quot;/app/server&quot;]        # good: the server gets SIGTERM\n# ENTRYPOINT /app/server           # shell form: a shell is PID 1 and may swallow it\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If the program needs longer than ten seconds to drain, raise the timeout with \u003ccode>docker stop -t 30\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>stop_grace_period\u003c/code> in Compose. If it is a script, use \u003ccode>exec\u003c/code> at the end so the shell is replaced by the program, or use a tiny init such as \u003ccode>tini\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>docker run --init\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"limits-health-and-restarts\">Limits, health and restarts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run -d --name shop \\\n    --memory 512m --cpus 1.0 --pids-limit 200 \\\n    --restart unless-stopped \\\n    --health-cmd &quot;curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/healthz || exit 1&quot; \\\n    --health-interval 15s --health-retries 3 \\\n    shop:1.4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker run -d --name shop \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --memory 512m --cpus 1.0 --pids-limit 200 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --restart unless-stopped \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --health-cmd \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/healthz || exit 1&quot;\u003c/span> \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    --health-interval 15s --health-retries 3 \\\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd cont\">    shop:1.4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The memory limit makes the kernel kill only this container if it grows without bound. The pids limit stops a fork bomb. A restart policy brings the container back after a crash or reboot, and a health check lets the platform see that a running process is no longer serving. Health checks should test the application, not just the port.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"logs-and-data\">Logs and data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write logs to standard output and standard error and let the runtime collect them; do not write log files inside the container. Set a log driver limit so the host disk cannot fill (\u003ccode>--log-opt max-size=10m --log-opt max-file=3\u003c/code>). Keep data that must survive in a \u003cstrong>volume\u003c/strong>, and treat the container filesystem as disposable. Pin image tags to a version or a digest, never \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code>, so a restart does not silently change the program.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A container takes exactly ten seconds to stop and logs &quot;Killed&quot;. What do you check first?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Whether the program is PID 1 and handles SIGTERM. The shell form of ENTRYPOINT or a wrapper script may hide the signal.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A process that ignores SIGTERM is killed by SIGKILL after the grace period.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-requests-lost-on-every-deploy\">Incident: Requests lost on every deploy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each rolling restart produced a burst of 502 errors for about ten seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker stop shop; echo $?\ndocker logs shop | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker stop shop; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> $?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">137\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker logs shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">listening on :8080\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(no shutdown message)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Exit status 137 means the container was killed. The Dockerfile used the shell form of \u003ccode>ENTRYPOINT\u003c/code>, so the application never received SIGTERM, and the in-flight requests were cut off. After switching to the exec form and adding a handler that stops accepting connections and drains for up to 20 seconds, the 502 bursts disappeared. The team also set a \u003ccode>stop_grace_period\u003c/code> of 30 seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running as root with a writable filesystem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using the shell form of ENTRYPOINT or CMD\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping resource limits and health checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing logs and data inside the container filesystem\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"reduce-what-a-container-may-do","text":"Reduce what a container may do"},{"id":"stop-gracefully","text":"Stop gracefully"},{"id":"limits-health-and-restarts","text":"Limits, health and restarts"},{"id":"logs-and-data","text":"Logs and data"},{"id":"incident-requests-lost-on-every-deploy","text":"Incident: Requests lost on every deploy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"docker run reference","u":"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/run/"},{"t":"Docker security","u":"https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/"}],"text":"A container that works on a laptop can still be a poor citizen in production. It runs as root, it ignores the stop signal and is killed after ten seconds mid-request, and it has no limits, so one bug takes the host down. A short checklist of run-time settings turns a working container into a dependable one. What you will learn Run as a non-root user with a read-only filesystem and dropped capabilities Handle SIGTERM for graceful shutdown Set resource limits, health checks and a restart policy Log to standard output and keep data in volumes Reduce what a container may do Containers share the host kernel, so you narrow their rights. Four settings do most of the work: $ docker run -d --name shop \\ --user 10001:10001 \\ --read-only --tmpfs /tmp \\ --cap-drop ALL \\ --security-opt no-new-privileges \\ shop:1.4 --user avoids root inside the container, --read-only stops an attacker from changing the program, --tmpfs /tmp provides the one writable place most programs need, and --cap-drop ALL removes Linux capabilities that a web service never uses. Add back only what you must, for example --cap-add NET_BIND_SERVICE to bind a port below 1024. Stop gracefully docker stop sends SIGTERM and waits ten seconds before SIGKILL. A service must catch SIGTERM, stop accepting new work, finish current requests and exit. In a Dockerfile, use the exec form so the program is process 1 and receives the signal: ENTRYPOINT [\"/app/server\"] # good: the server gets SIGTERM # ENTRYPOINT /app/server # shell form: a shell is PID 1 and may swallow it If the program needs longer than ten seconds to drain, raise the timeout with docker stop -t 30 or stop_grace_period in Compose. If it is a script, use exec at the end so the shell is replaced by the program, or use a tiny init such as tini ( docker run --init ). Limits, health and restarts $ docker run -d --name shop \\ --memory 512m --cpus 1.0 --pids-limit 200 \\ --restart unless-stopped \\ --health-cmd \"curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/healthz || exit 1\" \\ --health-interval 15s --health-retries 3 \\ shop:1.4 The memory limit makes the kernel kill only this container if it grows without bound. The pids limit stops a fork bomb. A restart policy brings the container back after a crash or reboot, and a health check lets the platform see that a running process is no longer serving. Health checks should test the application, not just the port. Logs and data Write logs to standard output and standard error and let the runtime collect them; do not write log files inside the container. Set a log driver limit so the host disk cannot fill ( --log-opt max-size=10m --log-opt max-file=3 ). Keep data that must survive in a volume , and treat the container filesystem as disposable. Pin image tags to a version or a digest, never latest , so a restart does not silently change the program. Predict first. A container takes exactly ten seconds to stop and logs \"Killed\". What do you check first? Your guess Answer: Whether the program is PID 1 and handles SIGTERM. The shell form of ENTRYPOINT or a wrapper script may hide the signal. A process that ignores SIGTERM is killed by SIGKILL after the grace period. Incident: Requests lost on every deploy Incident Each rolling restart produced a burst of 502 errors for about ten seconds. $ docker stop shop; echo $? 137 $ docker logs shop | tail -2 listening on :8080 (no shutdown message) Exit status 137 means the container was killed. The Dockerfile used the shell form of ENTRYPOINT , so the application never received SIGTERM, and the in-flight requests were cut off. After switching to the exec form and adding a handler that stops accepting connections and drains for up to 20 seconds, the 502 bursts disappeared. The team also set a stop_grace_period of 30 seconds. Common mistakes Running as root with a writable filesystem Using the shell form of ENTRYPOINT or CMD Skipping resource limits and health checks Writing logs and data inside the container filesystem"},"docker/debugging-containers":{"html":"\u003cp>A container that exits straight away, restarts in a loop, or behaves differently from your laptop frustrates people because it leaves little behind. In fact Docker keeps a lot of evidence: the exit code, the last logs, the configuration it actually used, and events. This lesson shows where each piece lives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading exit codes (0, 1, 125-127, 137, 139, 143)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>logs\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>inspect\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>events\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>stats\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>exec\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging images with no shell\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The usual causes of crash loops\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-the-facts\">Start with the facts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker ps -a --format 'table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}'\ndocker logs --tail 50 --timestamps shop\ndocker inspect shop --format '{{.State.ExitCode}} {{.State.OOMKilled}} {{.HostConfig.Memory}}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker ps -a --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAMES    STATUS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop     Restarting (137) 12 seconds ago\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker logs --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> 50 --timestamps shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect shop --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{.State.ExitCode}} {{.State.OOMKilled}} {{.HostConfig.Memory}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">137 true 268435456\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Exit code\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Usual meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Process finished normally\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>1\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application error\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>125\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Docker itself failed to run the command\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>126, 127\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Command not executable, or not found\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>137\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Killed (SIGKILL), often out of memory\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>139\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Segmentation fault\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>143\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stopped by SIGTERM\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Here 137 together with \u003ccode>OOMKilled true\u003c/code> and a 256 MB limit says the process used more memory than allowed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"get-inside\">Get inside\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker exec -it shop sh                       # shell in a running container\ndocker run --rm -it --entrypoint sh shop:1.4  # shell in the image, skipping the failing entrypoint\ndocker cp shop:/app/config.yml .              # copy a file out to read it\ndocker diff shop                              # what changed in the filesystem\ndocker stats --no-stream                      # CPU and memory now\ndocker events --since 10m                     # starts, dies, OOM and health events\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> -it shop sh                       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># shell in a running container\u003c/span>\ndocker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --entrypoint sh shop:1.4  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># shell in the image, skipping the failing entrypoint\u003c/span>\ndocker \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cp\u003c/span> shop:/app/config.yml .              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># copy a file out to read it\u003c/span>\ndocker diff shop                              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># what changed in the filesystem\u003c/span>\ndocker stats --no-stream                      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># CPU and memory now\u003c/span>\ndocker events --since 10m                     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># starts, dies, OOM and health events\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For minimal images with no shell, attach a debug container that shares the target&#39;s namespaces:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run --rm -it --pid=container:shop --net=container:shop nicolaka/netshoot\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it --pid=container:shop --net=container:shop nicolaka/netshoot\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"usual-causes-of-a-crash-loop\">Usual causes of a crash loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The main process exits (a script that ends, or a service that daemonises itself)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A missing environment variable or config file\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A port already in use, or binding to \u003ccode>127.0.0.1\u003c/code> so published ports do not work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The wrong user, so the app cannot write to a volume\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A health check that is too strict, so the orchestrator keeps killing a working container\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A container prints &quot;exec format error&quot; on start. What is the likely cause?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The image was built for a different CPU architecture, such as arm64 on an amd64 host.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Build with the matching platform or use a multi-architecture image (docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"status&gt;logs;status&gt;cfg;logs&gt;shell;cfg&gt;shell;shell&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From a dead container to a cause\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"status\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Exit code and state\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"logs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Logs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cfg\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Effective config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"shell\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shell inside\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix the image or run options\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Exit code and state to Logs; 2, Exit code and state to Effective config; 3, Logs to Shell inside; 4, Effective config to Shell inside; 5, Shell inside to Fix the image or run options.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"status\">\u003cdt>Exit code and state \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> docker ps -a and inspect give the code and whether it was OOM killed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Looking only at the restart count hides why it exits.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"logs\">\u003cdt>Logs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> docker logs shows the last output with timestamps.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Applications that log to files inside the container leave nothing here.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cfg\">\u003cdt>Effective config \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> inspect shows env, mounts, user, limits and command.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assuming the image defaults still apply misses overrides.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"shell\">\u003cdt>Shell inside \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> exec or run --entrypoint sh lets you test files and commands.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Minimal images lack a shell; use a debug container.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix the image or run options \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Change the cause, rebuild, and re-run with the same flags.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Fixing only in a running container is lost on the next start.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-container-that-restarted-every-minute\">Incident: the container that restarted every minute\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new image is released and the service restarts about once a minute. Logs show nothing unusual before each restart.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker inspect api --format '{{.State.OOMKilled}} {{.HostConfig.Memory}}'\ndocker stats --no-stream api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect api --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{.State.OOMKilled}} {{.HostConfig.Memory}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">true 536870912\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker stats --no-stream api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME   CPU %   MEM USAGE / LIMIT\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api    3.1%    508MiB / 512MiB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A new cache library kept results without a size limit. Memory grew until the 512 MB limit killed the process with code 137. The team bounded the cache, raised the limit with measured headroom, and added a memory alert.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running \u003ccode>docker logs\u003c/code> once, seeing nothing, and giving up (check the exit code and events)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging in a running container and forgetting to put the fix into the image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> tags, so nobody knows which image is running\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a restart policy as a fix\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-the-facts","text":"Start with the facts"},{"id":"get-inside","text":"Get inside"},{"id":"usual-causes-of-a-crash-loop","text":"Usual causes of a crash loop"},{"id":"incident-the-container-that-restarted-every-minute","text":"Incident: the container that restarted every minute"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Docker docs: docker container inspect","u":"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/inspect/"},{"t":"Docker docs: docker container logs","u":"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/logs/"}],"text":"A container that exits straight away, restarts in a loop, or behaves differently from your laptop frustrates people because it leaves little behind. In fact Docker keeps a lot of evidence: the exit code, the last logs, the configuration it actually used, and events. This lesson shows where each piece lives. What you will learn Reading exit codes (0, 1, 125-127, 137, 139, 143) Using logs , inspect , events , stats and exec Debugging images with no shell The usual causes of crash loops Start with the facts $ docker ps -a --format &#x27;table {{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}&#x27; NAMES STATUS shop Restarting (137) 12 seconds ago $ docker logs -- tail 50 --timestamps shop $ docker inspect shop --format &#x27;{{.State.ExitCode}} {{.State.OOMKilled}} {{.HostConfig.Memory}}&#x27; 137 true 268435456 Exit code Usual meaning 0 Process finished normally 1 Application error 125 Docker itself failed to run the command 126, 127 Command not executable, or not found 137 Killed (SIGKILL), often out of memory 139 Segmentation fault 143 Stopped by SIGTERM Here 137 together with OOMKilled true and a 256 MB limit says the process used more memory than allowed. Get inside docker exec -it shop sh # shell in a running container docker run -- rm -it --entrypoint sh shop:1.4 # shell in the image, skipping the failing entrypoint docker cp shop:/app/config.yml . # copy a file out to read it docker diff shop # what changed in the filesystem docker stats --no-stream # CPU and memory now docker events --since 10m # starts, dies, OOM and health events For minimal images with no shell, attach a debug container that shares the target&#39;s namespaces: docker run -- rm -it --pid=container:shop --net=container:shop nicolaka/netshoot Usual causes of a crash loop The main process exits (a script that ends, or a service that daemonises itself) A missing environment variable or config file A port already in use, or binding to 127.0.0.1 so published ports do not work The wrong user, so the app cannot write to a volume A health check that is too strict, so the orchestrator keeps killing a working container Predict first. A container prints \"exec format error\" on start. What is the likely cause? Your guess Answer: The image was built for a different CPU architecture, such as arm64 on an amd64 host. Build with the matching platform or use a multi-architecture image (docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64). From a dead container to a cause Flow in order: 1, Exit code and state to Logs; 2, Exit code and state to Effective config; 3, Logs to Shell inside; 4, Effective config to Shell inside; 5, Shell inside to Fix the image or run options. Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Exit code and state Step What it does: docker ps -a and inspect give the code and whether it was OOM killed. If it fails or is weak: Looking only at the restart count hides why it exits. Logs Observability What it does: docker logs shows the last output with timestamps. If it fails or is weak: Applications that log to files inside the container leave nothing here. Effective config Step What it does: inspect shows env, mounts, user, limits and command. If it fails or is weak: Assuming the image defaults still apply misses overrides. Shell inside Step What it does: exec or run --entrypoint sh lets you test files and commands. If it fails or is weak: Minimal images lack a shell; use a debug container. Fix the image or run options Step What it does: Change the cause, rebuild, and re-run with the same flags. If it fails or is weak: Fixing only in a running container is lost on the next start. Incident: the container that restarted every minute Incident A new image is released and the service restarts about once a minute. Logs show nothing unusual before each restart. $ docker inspect api --format &#x27;{{.State.OOMKilled}} {{.HostConfig.Memory}}&#x27; true 536870912 $ docker stats --no-stream api NAME CP"}});